/* WISEBYTE THINK & TEST CONTENT (part 3) */
WB.GK.push(
  {"cat":"geography","q":"Which river flows through Baghdad, the capital of Iraq?","o":["Tigris","Euphrates","Jordan","Indus"],"a":0,"e":"Baghdad was founded beside the Tigris in 762 as the grand new capital of the Abbasid caliphs."},
  {"cat":"geography","q":"What is the longest river in Europe?","o":["Danube","Volga","Rhine","Dnieper"],"a":1,"e":"The Volga runs about 3,500 km through Russia and empties into the Caspian Sea, which has no outlet to the ocean."},
  {"cat":"geography","q":"Which country is home to the Atacama Desert, one of the driest places on Earth?","o":["Peru","Argentina","Chile","Mexico"],"a":2,"e":"Its dry, clear skies make it one of the best places in the world for astronomers to build large telescopes."},
  {"cat":"geography","q":"The Danube River empties into which sea?","o":["Adriatic Sea","Baltic Sea","North Sea","Black Sea"],"a":3,"e":"The Danube passes through or along ten countries, more than any other river in the world."},
  {"cat":"geography","q":"Which continent has no permanent human population?","o":["Antarctica","Australia","Greenland","Arctic"],"a":0,"e":"Only scientists and support staff live there, rising to a few thousand in summer and dropping sharply in winter.","k":1},
  {"cat":"geography","q":"Mount Kosciuszko, the highest mountain on the Australian mainland, is in which state?","o":["Victoria","New South Wales","Tasmania","Queensland"],"a":1,"e":"Polish explorer Paweł Strzelecki named it in 1840 after the Polish-Lithuanian hero Tadeusz Kościuszko.","k":1},
  {"cat":"geography","q":"Which strait separates Asia from North America?","o":["Strait of Malacca","Bass Strait","Bering Strait","Strait of Hormuz"],"a":2,"e":"At its narrowest it is only about 82 km wide, and two small islands in the middle sit on opposite sides of the International Date Line."},
  {"cat":"geography","q":"Which line of latitude passes through the Queensland city of Rockhampton?","o":["Equator","Tropic of Cancer","Antarctic Circle","Tropic of Capricorn"],"a":3,"e":"The Tropic slowly shifts as Earth's tilt changes, so Rockhampton's marker spire no longer sits exactly on the line."},
  {"cat":"geography","q":"The city of Istanbul sits on both sides of which strait, dividing Europe from Asia?","o":["Bosphorus","Dardanelles","Strait of Gibraltar","Suez Canal"],"a":0,"e":"The Bosphorus links the Black Sea to the Sea of Marmara, and ships have used it for thousands of years."},
  {"cat":"geography","q":"Which small kingdom is completely surrounded by South Africa?","o":["Eswatini","Lesotho","Botswana","Namibia"],"a":1,"e":"Every part of Lesotho lies above about 1,400 m, giving it the highest lowest point of any country."},
  {"cat":"geography","q":"Which mountain range forms the border between France and Spain?","o":["Alps","Apennines","Pyrenees","Carpathians"],"a":2,"e":"The tiny country of Andorra is tucked high in the Pyrenees between the two.","k":1},
  {"cat":"geography","q":"Which sea lies between Australia and New Zealand?","o":["Coral Sea","Timor Sea","Arafura Sea","Tasman Sea"],"a":3,"e":"It is named after Dutch explorer Abel Tasman, the first European known to reach New Zealand, in 1642.","k":1},
  {"cat":"geography","q":"Not counting continental Australia, what is the world's largest island?","o":["Greenland","New Guinea","Borneo","Madagascar"],"a":0,"e":"Most of Greenland is buried under an ice sheet that is up to about 3 km thick.","k":1},
  {"cat":"geography","q":"What is the Pacific 'Ring of Fire'?","o":["A coral reef system around Polynesia","A horseshoe-shaped belt of volcanoes and earthquake zones","A pattern of ocean currents","A chain of undersea hot springs near Hawaii"],"a":1,"e":"It traces the edges of tectonic plates around the Pacific, where most of the world's earthquakes happen."},
  {"cat":"geography","q":"Victoria Falls lies on which African river?","o":["Nile","Congo","Zambezi","Limpopo"],"a":2,"e":"Locally it is called Mosi-oa-Tunya, meaning 'the smoke that thunders', after its huge clouds of spray."},
  {"cat":"geography","q":"What is the name of the line of 0° longitude?","o":["Equator","International Date Line","Tropic of Cancer","Prime Meridian"],"a":3,"e":"An international conference in 1884 chose the line through the Royal Observatory at Greenwich, London."},
  {"cat":"geography","q":"Lake Titicaca, one of the highest large lakes in the world, lies on the border of Peru and which country?","o":["Bolivia","Chile","Ecuador","Colombia"],"a":0,"e":"It sits about 3,800 m above sea level, and the Uros people live on floating islands woven from reeds."},
  {"cat":"geography","q":"The shore of which body of water is the lowest point on dry land?","o":["Caspian Sea","Dead Sea","Lake Eyre","Death Valley's Badwater Basin"],"a":1,"e":"Its shore is more than 400 m below sea level and keeps getting lower as the water level falls."},
  {"cat":"geography","q":"Which country contains most of the Amazon rainforest?","o":["Peru","Colombia","Brazil","Venezuela"],"a":2,"e":"Around 60% of the Amazon lies in Brazil, with the rest spread across eight other countries and territories.","k":1},
  {"cat":"geography","q":"What is a fjord?","o":["A flat-topped hill","A volcanic crater lake","A sandbar across a river mouth","A long, narrow sea inlet carved by a glacier"],"a":3,"e":"Milford Sound in New Zealand is actually a fjord, despite its name."},
  {"cat":"geography","q":"Which river forms much of the border between the United States and Mexico?","o":["Rio Grande","Colorado River","Mississippi River","Red River"],"a":0,"e":"In Mexico the same river is called the Río Bravo del Norte."},
  {"cat":"geography","q":"Darwin is closer to which Asian capital than it is to Canberra?","o":["Tokyo","Jakarta","Beijing","New Delhi"],"a":1,"e":"Jakarta is about 2,700 km from Darwin, while Canberra is more than 3,000 km away."},
  {"cat":"geography","q":"What is the largest of North America's Great Lakes?","o":["Lake Michigan","Lake Huron","Lake Superior","Lake Ontario"],"a":2,"e":"Lake Superior is so big it could hold all the water of the other four Great Lakes, with room to spare."},
  {"cat":"geography","q":"The name 'Nullarbor' comes from Latin. What does it mean?","o":["Dry land","Red earth","Endless plain","No tree"],"a":3,"e":"The Eyre Highway across the Nullarbor includes a 146.6 km stretch with no curves, Australia's longest straight road."},
  {"cat":"geography","q":"Which two countries share the longest international border in the world?","o":["Canada and the United States","Russia and China","Argentina and Chile","Russia and Kazakhstan"],"a":0,"e":"Including the Alaska section, the border runs for about 8,900 km."},
  {"cat":"geography","q":"Mount Everest sits on the border between Nepal and which other country?","o":["India","China","Bhutan","Pakistan"],"a":1,"e":"A joint Nepal-China survey in 2020 measured its height at 8,848.86 m."},
  {"cat":"geography","q":"Which Asian country is made up of more than 7,000 islands, with Manila as its capital?","o":["Indonesia","Japan","Philippines","Malaysia"],"a":2,"e":"The islands are commonly grouped into three main regions: Luzon, the Visayas and Mindanao."},
  {"cat":"history","q":"In 1770, which navigator charted Australia's east coast aboard HMS Endeavour?","o":["Abel Tasman","Matthew Flinders","William Dampier","James Cook"],"a":3,"e":"He first landed at Kamay (Botany Bay), named for the many new plants his botanists collected there.","k":1},
  {"cat":"history","q":"Genghis Khan founded which empire?","o":["Ottoman Empire","Mongol Empire","Persian Empire","Mughal Empire"],"a":1,"e":"At its height it became the largest connected land empire in history, stretching from Korea to Eastern Europe.","k":1},
  {"cat":"history","q":"The Rosetta Stone was the key to reading which ancient writing?","o":["Babylonian cuneiform","Mayan glyphs","Egyptian hieroglyphs","Linear A"],"a":2,"e":"It carries the same decree in hieroglyphs, Demotic script and ancient Greek, and Jean-François Champollion cracked it in 1822."},
  {"cat": "history", "q": "Which Australian prime minister disappeared while swimming at Cheviot Beach, Victoria, in December 1967?", "o": ["Robert Menzies", "John Curtin", "Harold Holt", "Joseph Lyons"], "a": 2, "e": "He had been prime minister for less than two years, and his body was never found."},
  {"cat":"history","q":"Cleopatra VII, the last active ruler of Ptolemaic Egypt, belonged to a royal family of what origin?","o":["Macedonian Greek","Nubian","Roman","Persian"],"a":0,"e":"Her family had ruled Egypt for nearly 300 years, yet she is said to be the first of them to learn the Egyptian language."},
  {"cat":"history","q":"Around 1440, who developed a printing press with movable metal type in Europe?","o":["William Caxton","Johannes Gutenberg","Leonardo da Vinci","Martin Luther"],"a":1,"e":"Movable type already existed in China and Korea, but Gutenberg's press spread rapidly and transformed European learning."},
  {"cat":"history","q":"Which Roman city was buried by the eruption of Mount Vesuvius in 79 CE?","o":["Carthage","Ostia","Syracuse","Pompeii"],"a":3,"e":"The ash preserved the town so well that archaeologists have found bread still in its ovens.","k":1},
  {"cat":"history","q":"The Gallipoli peninsula, where Anzac troops landed on 25 April 1915, is in which modern country?","o":["Turkey","Greece","Egypt","Bulgaria"],"a":0,"e":"The date of the landing is now marked each year as Anzac Day in Australia and New Zealand.","k":1},
  {"cat":"history","q":"Who was the first President of the United States?","o":["Thomas Jefferson","George Washington","Abraham Lincoln","John Adams"],"a":1,"e":"He is the only president who never lived in the White House, which was finished after his term ended.","k":1},
  {"cat":"history","q":"In 1453, Constantinople fell to which empire?","o":["Mongol Empire","Holy Roman Empire","Ottoman Empire","Persian Empire"],"a":2,"e":"Sultan Mehmed II used enormous cannons to break the city's ancient walls, ending the Byzantine Empire."},
  {"cat":"history","q":"Which English king agreed to the Magna Carta in 1215?","o":["Henry VIII","Richard the Lionheart","William the Conqueror","King John"],"a":3,"e":"A few of its clauses, including the right to justice without delay, are still part of English law."},
  {"cat":"history","q":"Mahatma Gandhi's 1930 Salt March was a protest against which ruling power in India?","o":["The British","The Portuguese","The Mughals","The French"],"a":0,"e":"He and his followers walked about 390 km to the sea to make salt, defying a British tax on it."},
  {"cat":"history","q":"In what year did the First Fleet arrive at Sydney Cove?","o":["1770","1788","1801","1824"],"a":1,"e":"More than 1,000 people made the voyage of about eight months from England in eleven ships.","k":1},
  {"cat":"history","q":"The Terracotta Army was buried to guard the tomb of which Chinese ruler?","o":["Kublai Khan","Emperor Wu of Han","Qin Shi Huang","Sun Yat-sen"],"a":2,"e":"Farmers digging a well near Xi'an stumbled upon it in 1974, and the soldiers' faces vary so much that no two seem alike."},
  {"cat":"history","q":"In what year did Nelson Mandela become President of South Africa?","o":["1976","1990","1994","2000"],"a":2,"e":"He was elected after spending 27 years in prison, and won the Nobel Peace Prize in 1993."},
  {"cat":"history","q":"How long did the Hundred Years' War between England and France actually last?","o":["116 years","100 years","99 years","132 years"],"a":0,"e":"It ran from 1337 to 1453, with long pauses between the fighting."},
  {"cat":"history","q":"Which ancient people invented cuneiform, one of the earliest writing systems?","o":["Egyptians","Sumerians","Phoenicians","Minoans"],"a":1,"e":"They wrote by pressing a wedge-shaped reed into wet clay, and 'cuneiform' means 'wedge-shaped'."},
  {"cat":"history","q":"Who led the first expedition to reach the South Pole, in 1911?","o":["Robert Falcon Scott","Ernest Shackleton","Roald Amundsen","Douglas Mawson"],"a":2,"e":"His Norwegian team used dog sleds and beat Scott's British party by about five weeks."},
  {"cat":"history","q":"In 1893, which country became the first self-governing nation to give women the vote in national elections?","o":["Australia","United Kingdom","Finland","New Zealand"],"a":3,"e":"South Australia soon went a step further, passing a law in 1894 that also let women stand for parliament."},
  {"cat":"history","q":"The first voyage around the world, from 1519 to 1522, was led at the start by which explorer, who died before it ended?","o":["Ferdinand Magellan","Vasco da Gama","Christopher Columbus","Francis Drake"],"a":0,"e":"Magellan was killed in the Philippines, and Juan Sebastián Elcano brought the last ship home to Spain."},
  {"cat":"history","q":"Which people built Tenochtitlan, the city on whose ruins Mexico City now stands?","o":["Maya","Aztecs (Mexica)","Inca","Olmecs"],"a":1,"e":"It was built on an island in Lake Texcoco and linked to the shore by long causeways."},
  {"cat":"history","q":"The Black Death, which swept Europe from 1347, was caused by what?","o":["A flu virus","Contaminated drinking water","Plague bacteria spread largely by fleas","A fungus in grain"],"a":2,"e":"It killed perhaps a third or more of Europe's population within a few years."},
  {"cat":"history","q":"Which country has more ancient pyramids than Egypt?","o":["Ethiopia","Greece","Libya","Sudan"],"a":3,"e":"The kingdom of Kush built over 200 steep-sided pyramids there, many at the royal city of Meroë."},
  {"cat":"history","q":"The Kokoda Track campaign of 1942 was fought in which present-day country?","o":["Papua New Guinea","Indonesia","Solomon Islands","Malaysia"],"a":0,"e":"Australian troops were supported by Papuan carriers who carried supplies and the wounded over rugged mountains."},
  {"cat":"history","q":"Which British monarch reigned from 1837 to 1901, giving her name to an era?","o":["Queen Elizabeth I","Queen Victoria","Queen Anne","Queen Mary II"],"a":1,"e":"Both the state of Victoria and the state of Queensland were named in her honour."},
  {"cat":"science","q":"What is the chemical symbol for iron?","o":["Ir","In","Fe","I"],"a":2,"e":"It comes from 'ferrum', the Latin word for iron, which also gives us the word 'ferrous'."},
  {"cat":"science","q":"Which gas do plants take in from the air to make food through photosynthesis?","o":["Oxygen","Nitrogen","Helium","Carbon dioxide"],"a":3,"e":"The oxygen plants give off actually comes from splitting water molecules, not from the carbon dioxide.","k":1},
  {"cat":"science","q":"Which gas gives the classic red-orange glow of old advertising signs?","o":["Neon","Argon","Hydrogen","Chlorine"],"a":0,"e":"Other gases in the tubes make other colours, and the name comes from the Greek word for 'new'."},
  {"cat":"science","q":"What does DNA stand for?","o":["Dinitrogen acid","Deoxyribonucleic acid","Deoxyribose nuclear atom","Double nucleic arrangement"],"a":1,"e":"Unwound, the DNA in just one of your cells would stretch about 2 metres."},
  {"cat":"science","q":"Roughly how fast does light travel in a vacuum?","o":["About 300 km per second","About 30,000 km per second","About 300,000 km per second","About 3 million km per second"],"a":2,"e":"That is fast enough to circle Earth about seven and a half times in one second."},
  {"cat": "science", "q": "Who discovered X-rays in 1895?", "o": ["Wilhelm Röntgen", "Marie Curie", "Thomas Edison", "Nikola Tesla"], "a": 0, "e": "One of his first X-ray pictures showed the bones of his wife's hand, wedding ring and all. He won the first Nobel Prize in Physics in 1901."},
  {"cat":"science","q":"What type of rock forms when lava or magma cools and hardens?","o":["Igneous","Sedimentary","Metamorphic","Limestone"],"a":0,"e":"Granite and basalt are both igneous rocks, and the word comes from the Latin for 'fire'."},
  {"cat":"science","q":"Which metal is a liquid at room temperature?","o":["Lead","Mercury","Tin","Aluminium"],"a":1,"e":"Gallium comes close: it melts at about 30°C, so it can turn liquid in your hand.","k":1},
  {"cat":"science","q":"What is the SI unit of electrical resistance?","o":["Volt","Watt","Ohm","Ampere"],"a":2,"e":"It is named after German physicist Georg Ohm, and its symbol is the Greek letter omega (Ω)."},
  {"cat": "science", "q": "Which Adelaide-educated scientist shared the 1915 Nobel Prize in Physics with his father, at just 25?", "o": ["Howard Florey", "Lawrence Bragg", "Ernest Rutherford", "Mark Oliphant"], "a": 1, "e": "The Braggs used X-rays to work out how atoms are arranged in crystals. He is still the youngest person to win the physics prize."},
  {"cat":"science","q":"What colour does blue litmus paper turn in an acid?","o":["Red","Green","Yellow","Purple"],"a":0,"e":"Litmus dye is made from lichens and has been used to test acids for centuries.","k":1},
  {"cat":"science","q":"In science, what is a 'half-life'?","o":["Half the lifespan of a living cell","The time for half of a radioactive sample to decay","The time a battery takes to lose half its charge","Half the time an element takes to melt"],"a":1,"e":"Carbon-14 has a half-life of about 5,730 years, which is why it is used to date ancient remains."},
  {"cat":"science","q":"What is the chemical formula for table salt?","o":["KCl","NaOH","NaCl","CaCO₃"],"a":2,"e":"It is made of sodium, a metal that reacts violently with water, and chlorine, a poisonous gas."},
  {"cat":"science","q":"The ozone layer, which absorbs harmful UV rays, is found in which layer of the atmosphere?","o":["Troposphere","Mesosphere","Thermosphere","Stratosphere"],"a":3,"e":"After the 1987 Montreal Protocol phased out ozone-damaging chemicals, the ozone hole has slowly begun to recover."},
  {"cat":"science","q":"Which colour of visible light has the longest wavelength?","o":["Red","Violet","Blue","Green"],"a":0,"e":"Just beyond red is infrared, which we can't see but can feel as heat.","k":1},
  {"cat": "science", "q": "Which scientist coined the word 'cell' in 1665 after looking at thin slices of cork under a microscope?", "o": ["Robert Hooke", "Antonie van Leeuwenhoek", "Isaac Newton", "Galileo Galilei"], "a": 0, "e": "The tiny boxes reminded him of the small rooms, or cells, that monks lived in."},
  {"cat":"science","q":"Bronze is an alloy made mainly from copper and which other metal?","o":["Iron","Zinc","Tin","Silver"],"a":2,"e":"It was so important it gave its name to a whole era, the Bronze Age."},
  {"cat":"science","q":"What is it called when a solid turns directly into a gas without becoming a liquid?","o":["Condensation","Evaporation","Melting","Sublimation"],"a":3,"e":"Dry ice (frozen carbon dioxide) does this, which is why it makes spooky fog instead of puddles."},
  {"cat":"science","q":"Which particle in an atom's nucleus has no electric charge?","o":["Neutron","Proton","Electron","Ion"],"a":0,"e":"James Chadwick discovered the neutron in 1932, and won the Nobel Prize for it three years later."},
  {"cat":"science","q":"What does a seismometer measure?","o":["Wind speed","Ground shaking, such as from earthquakes","Rainfall","Air pressure"],"a":1,"e":"Seismometers are so sensitive they can detect large earthquakes on the other side of the world.","k":1},
  {"cat":"science","q":"In Einstein's equation E = mc², what does 'c' stand for?","o":["The mass of an object","A constant of gravity","The speed of light","The charge of an electron"],"a":2,"e":"Because c is squared, a tiny amount of mass holds an enormous amount of energy."},
  {"cat":"science","q":"Australians Barry Marshall and Robin Warren won a Nobel Prize for showing that most stomach ulcers are caused by what?","o":["Stress","Spicy food","Too much stomach acid alone","A bacterium"],"a":3,"e":"To prove it, Marshall drank a culture of the bacteria and soon developed stomach inflammation."},
  {"cat":"science","q":"Why can't sound travel through outer space?","o":["There is no air or other matter to carry it","It is too cold","Light blocks it","Gravity pulls it back"],"a":0,"e":"Sound is a vibration passed from particle to particle, so in a near-vacuum there is nothing to vibrate.","k":1},
  {"cat":"science","q":"Which scientist described three famous laws of motion in 1687?","o":["Galileo Galilei","Isaac Newton","Albert Einstein","Johannes Kepler"],"a":1,"e":"He published them in a book usually called the Principia, which also set out his law of gravity.","k":1},
  {"cat":"science","q":"What is ordinary glass mainly made from?","o":["Salt","Clay","Sand","Chalk"],"a":2,"e":"The silica in sand melts at around 1,700°C, so soda is added to lower the melting point."},
  {"cat":"science","q":"In chemistry, what does a catalyst do?","o":["Stops a reaction completely","Changes a liquid into a gas","Makes a reaction produce more heat","Speeds up a reaction without being used up"],"a":3,"e":"Enzymes in your body are natural catalysts, and cars use catalytic converters to clean exhaust."},
  {"cat":"science","q":"What kind of energy is stored in a stretched rubber band?","o":["Elastic potential energy","Chemical energy","Nuclear energy","Sound energy"],"a":0,"e":"When you let go, that stored energy turns into movement energy as the band flies off.","k":1},
  {"cat": "technology", "q": "What was the first video ever uploaded to YouTube, in April 2005?", "o": ["A cat playing the piano", "A co-founder at the zoo talking about elephants", "A music video", "A skateboarding trick"], "a": 1, "e": "Titled 'Me at the zoo', the 19-second clip shows co-founder Jawed Karim at San Diego Zoo."},
  {"cat":"technology","q":"What does 'RAM' stand for in computing?","o":["Read And Modify","Rapid Application Module","Random Access Memory","Remote Access Mode"],"a":2,"e":"RAM is temporary working memory, so everything in it is lost when the power goes off."},
  {"cat":"technology","q":"Which Australian research organisation developed technology that became key to fast Wi-Fi?","o":["ANSTO","Telstra","Australian Antarctic Division","CSIRO"],"a":3,"e":"Its Wi-Fi patents earned it hundreds of millions of dollars in licence fees from tech companies."},
  {"cat":"technology","q":"Australian scientist David Warren invented which device for aircraft?","o":["The flight data recorder ('black box')","The jet engine","Radar","The ejection seat"],"a":0,"e":"Despite the nickname, flight recorders are painted bright orange so they are easier to find after a crash."},
  {"cat":"technology","q":"Who is often called the first computer programmer, for her 1840s notes on Charles Babbage's Analytical Engine?","o":["Grace Hopper","Ada Lovelace","Marie Curie","Hedy Lamarr"],"a":1,"e":"She also imagined that such machines might one day create music, not just do sums."},
  {"cat":"technology","q":"What does 'GPS' stand for?","o":["General Power Supply","Ground Position Sensor","Global Positioning System","Global Phone Service"],"a":2,"e":"Your device works out where it is using timing signals from at least four satellites at once.","k":1},
  {"cat":"technology","q":"What does 'USB' stand for?","o":["Unified System Board","Universal Storage Box","User Signal Bridge","Universal Serial Bus"],"a":3,"e":"USB was introduced in the mid-1990s to replace a tangle of different plugs and ports."},
  {"cat":"technology","q":"Which company created the Windows operating system?","o":["Microsoft","Apple","IBM","Google"],"a":0,"e":"Microsoft was founded in 1975 by childhood friends Bill Gates and Paul Allen."},
  {"cat": "technology", "q": "What does 'PDF' stand for?", "o": ["Printable Data File", "Portable Document Format", "Public Digital Form", "Page Display Format"], "a": 1, "e": "Adobe launched it in 1993 so a document looks the same on any device or printer.", "k": 1},
  {"cat":"technology","q":"What does 'LED' stand for?","o":["Low-energy display","Laser electric device","Light-emitting diode","Light electric dimmer"],"a":2,"e":"The 2014 Nobel Prize in Physics went to the inventors of the blue LED, which made bright white LED lighting possible."},
  {"cat":"technology","q":"Who was granted the first patent for the telephone, in 1876?","o":["Thomas Edison","Guglielmo Marconi","Nikola Tesla","Alexander Graham Bell"],"a":3,"e":"His first words on it were reportedly 'Mr Watson, come here, I want to see you.'","k":1},
  {"cat":"technology","q":"What is 'open-source' software?","o":["Software whose code anyone can view, use and change","Software that is free to download but locked","Software that only runs online","Software made by the government"],"a":0,"e":"Linux, which powers most of the world's web servers and Android phones, is open source."},
  {"cat":"technology","q":"What does 'QR' stand for in 'QR code'?","o":["Quality Record","Quick Response","Quick Reader","Query Result"],"a":1,"e":"QR codes were invented in Japan in 1994 to track car parts in factories."},
  {"cat": "technology", "q": "What was the first item sold on eBay, back when it was called AuctionWeb in 1995?", "o": ["A signed baseball", "A broken laser pointer", "A used car", "A Beanie Baby"], "a": 1, "e": "It sold for US$14.83. When founder Pierre Omidyar checked, the buyer said he collected broken laser pointers."},
  {"cat":"technology","q":"What does 'Moore's law' predict?","o":["Computers become half as expensive every year","Internet speed doubles every month","Batteries last twice as long every decade","The number of transistors on a chip doubles about every two years"],"a":3,"e":"Gordon Moore, co-founder of Intel, made the original observation in 1965."},
  {"cat":"technology","q":"Which inventor is famous for pioneering alternating current (AC) electricity systems?","o":["Nikola Tesla","Thomas Edison","Michael Faraday","James Watt"],"a":0,"e":"The SI unit of magnetic flux density, the tesla, is named in his honour."},
  {"cat":"technology","q":"What does 'SIM' stand for in 'SIM card'?","o":["Secure Internet Mode","Subscriber Identity Module","Signal Input Microchip","System Identity Memory"],"a":1,"e":"The card stores the details that let your mobile network recognise your account."},
  {"cat":"technology","q":"What is 'cloud computing'?","o":["Computers that measure the weather","Wireless charging","Storing and running things on remote servers over the internet","A type of screen saver"],"a":2,"e":"'The cloud' is really huge data centres full of servers, and some are built in cool climates to help keep them cool."},
  {"cat":"technology","q":"Which symbol did Ray Tomlinson choose for email addresses when he sent the first network email in 1971?","o":["#","&","%","@"],"a":3,"e":"He chose it because it was rarely used and neatly meant 'at' a particular computer."},
  {"cat":"technology","q":"Which Scottish inventor gave the first public demonstration of a working television in 1926?","o":["John Logie Baird","Alexander Fleming","James Watt","Alexander Graham Bell"],"a":0,"e":"His early television was mechanical, using a spinning disc with holes to scan the picture."},
  {"cat":"technology","q":"Australian Graeme Clark pioneered which medical device?","o":["The pacemaker","The cochlear implant (bionic ear)","The heart-lung machine","The X-ray machine"],"a":1,"e":"The first implant of his multi-channel device took place in Melbourne in 1978."},
  {"cat":"technology","q":"What is a 'pixel'?","o":["A type of computer virus","A unit of internet speed","One of the tiny dots that make up a digital image","A small computer chip"],"a":2,"e":"The word is short for 'picture element'.","k":1},
  {"cat":"technology","q":"In mobile networks like 4G and 5G, what does the 'G' stand for?","o":["Gigabyte","Global","Gateway","Generation"],"a":3,"e":"The first generation of mobile phones in the 1980s could only make voice calls."},
  {"cat":"technology","q":"What is two-factor authentication?","o":["Using a second proof of identity, like a code, as well as your password","Having two separate passwords","Logging in from two devices","Changing your password twice a year"],"a":0,"e":"Even if someone steals your password, they usually can't get in without the second factor."},
  {"cat":"technology","q":"What does 'HTML' stand for?","o":["High Tech Modern Language","HyperText Markup Language","Home Tool Markup Language","Hyperlink Text Management Logic"],"a":1,"e":"HTML describes the structure of web pages, while CSS controls how they look."},
  {"cat":"technology","q":"During World War II, what did Germany's Enigma machine do?","o":["Detected aircraft","Guided missiles","Encrypted secret messages","Jammed radio signals"],"a":2,"e":"Codebreakers at Bletchley Park, including Alan Turing, cracked its codes and shortened the war."},
  {"cat":"technology","q":"Which company released the Walkman portable cassette player in 1979?","o":["Panasonic","Philips","Toshiba","Sony"],"a":3,"e":"It changed music forever by letting people carry their own soundtrack wherever they went."},
  {"cat":"technology","q":"The word 'modem' is short for what?","o":["Modulator-demodulator","Mobile data manager","Modern device emulator","Multiple data monitor"],"a":0,"e":"A modem turns digital data into signals that can travel over phone or cable lines, and back again."},
  {"cat":"space","q":"Which planet is the hottest in our solar system?","o":["Mercury","Venus","Mars","Jupiter"],"a":1,"e":"Its thick carbon dioxide atmosphere traps heat, keeping the surface around 465°C, hotter than Mercury.","k":1},
  {"cat":"space","q":"What was the name of the first artificial satellite, launched in 1957?","o":["Explorer 1","Vostok 1","Sputnik 1","Telstar"],"a":2,"e":"It was a metal ball about 58 cm wide that sent out simple radio beeps anyone could pick up."},
  {"cat":"space","q":"Who was the first person to travel into space, in 1961?","o":["Neil Armstrong","John Glenn","Alan Shepard","Yuri Gagarin"],"a":3,"e":"His flight aboard Vostok 1 made one orbit of Earth and lasted about 108 minutes.","k":1},
  {"cat":"space","q":"Which planet rotates on its side, with an axis tilted about 98°?","o":["Uranus","Neptune","Saturn","Venus"],"a":0,"e":"Because of this tilt, each pole gets about 42 years of sunlight followed by 42 years of darkness."},
  {"cat":"space","q":"What does a light-year measure?","o":["Time","Distance","Brightness","Speed"],"a":1,"e":"It is the distance light travels in a year, about 9.46 trillion km.","k":1},
  {"cat":"space","q":"Which NSW radio telescope helped broadcast TV pictures of the Apollo 11 moonwalk to the world in 1969?","o":["Narrabri","Siding Spring","Parkes","Mount Stromlo"],"a":2,"e":"The Honeysuckle Creek station near Canberra carried the very first steps, then Parkes took over with clearer pictures."},
  {"cat":"space","q":"What is Jupiter's Great Red Spot?","o":["A huge volcano","A crater","A frozen lake","A giant storm"],"a":3,"e":"The storm is wider than Earth and has been watched through telescopes for well over 150 years.","k":1},
  {"cat":"space","q":"Triton, a large moon that orbits in the opposite direction to its planet's spin, belongs to which planet?","o":["Neptune","Uranus","Saturn","Jupiter"],"a":0,"e":"Its backwards orbit suggests Triton was captured from the Kuiper Belt rather than forming alongside Neptune."},
  {"cat": "space", "q": "Which planet has the most known moons?", "o": ["Jupiter", "Saturn", "Uranus", "Neptune"], "a": 1, "e": "Saturn has well over 200 confirmed moons, far more than Jupiter, and astronomers keep finding small new ones."},
  {"cat":"space","q":"What is the largest moon in the solar system?","o":["Titan","Callisto","Ganymede","Earth's Moon"],"a":2,"e":"Ganymede, which orbits Jupiter, is bigger than the planet Mercury."},
  {"cat":"space","q":"What causes Earth's seasons?","o":["Earth's changing distance from the Sun","The Moon's gravity","Changes in the Sun's brightness","The tilt of Earth's axis"],"a":3,"e":"Earth is actually closest to the Sun in early January, during Australia's summer and the northern winter.","k":1},
  {"cat":"space","q":"Why do we always see the same side of the Moon from Earth?","o":["It spins once for every orbit of Earth","It does not spin at all","Earth's shadow hides the other side","The other side has no light"],"a":0,"e":"This is called tidal locking, and the far side gets just as much sunlight as the near side."},
  {"cat":"space","q":"What is the Sun mostly made of?","o":["Helium","Hydrogen","Oxygen","Iron"],"a":1,"e":"Every second the Sun fuses around 600 million tonnes of hydrogen into helium."},
  {"cat":"space","q":"Which space telescope, launched in 2021, has a gold-coated mirror made of 18 hexagonal segments?","o":["Hubble Space Telescope","Kepler Space Telescope","James Webb Space Telescope","Spitzer Space Telescope"],"a":2,"e":"Its main mirror is 6.5 m across, so it had to fold up to fit inside its rocket."},
  {"cat":"space","q":"How many astronauts walked on the Moon during the Apollo missions from 1969 to 1972?","o":["2","6","12","24"],"a":2,"e":"Six Apollo missions landed, each carrying two moonwalkers while a third astronaut stayed in orbit."},
  {"cat":"space","q":"What is the Kuiper Belt?","o":["A region of icy objects beyond Neptune","A ring around Saturn","The belt of asteroids between Mars and Jupiter","A band of stars in the Milky Way"],"a":0,"e":"Pluto is one of its best-known members, and many short-period comets come from there."},
  {"cat":"space","q":"In 2006, what was Pluto reclassified as?","o":["A moon","A dwarf planet","An asteroid","A comet"],"a":1,"e":"Pluto was discovered in 1930 by American astronomer Clyde Tombaugh, who was just 24 at the time.","k":1},
  {"cat":"space","q":"Olympus Mons, one of the tallest volcanoes known in the solar system, is on which planet?","o":["Venus","Earth","Mars","Mercury"],"a":2,"e":"It rises about 22 km, roughly two and a half times the height of Mount Everest."},
  {"cat":"space","q":"Which way does a comet's tail point?","o":["Towards the Sun","Behind it along its path","Towards Earth","Away from the Sun"],"a":3,"e":"The Sun's radiation and the solar wind push the gas and dust outwards, so the tail can even lead the comet."},
  {"cat":"space","q":"Who was the first woman to travel into space, in 1963?","o":["Valentina Tereshkova","Sally Ride","Svetlana Savitskaya","Mae Jemison"],"a":0,"e":"She orbited Earth 48 times over nearly three days aboard Vostok 6.","k":1},
  {"cat":"space","q":"Launched in 1977, which spacecraft became the first to reach interstellar space in 2012?","o":["Pioneer 10","Voyager 1","New Horizons","Cassini"],"a":1,"e":"It carries a gold-plated record with sounds and images from Earth, in case anyone ever finds it."},
  {"cat":"space","q":"What is a supernova?","o":["A newly formed planet","A very bright comet","A huge explosion of a star","A cluster of young stars"],"a":2,"e":"Supernova 1987A, in the Large Magellanic Cloud, was easily seen from the Southern Hemisphere."},
  {"cat": "space", "q": "Which planet takes the shortest time to orbit the Sun?", "o": ["Venus", "Mercury", "Mars", "Earth"], "a": 1, "e": "A year on Mercury lasts just 88 Earth days, so you'd have a birthday about every three months."},
  {"cat":"space","q":"What is the official name of the constellation known as the Southern Cross?","o":["Crux","Centaurus","Orion","Carina"],"a":0,"e":"It is the smallest of the 88 official constellations, yet it appears on the Australian flag."},
  {"cat":"space","q":"Roughly how long does the Moon take to go through its full cycle of phases?","o":["About a day","About a month","About a week","About a year"],"a":1,"e":"The full cycle takes about 29.5 days, and the word 'month' comes from 'moon'.","k":1},
  {"cat":"space","q":"The main asteroid belt lies between which two planets?","o":["Earth and Mars","Jupiter and Saturn","Mars and Jupiter","Venus and Earth"],"a":2,"e":"Despite films showing crowded rocks, the asteroids are so spread out that spacecraft pass through easily.","k":1},
  {"cat":"space","q":"Roughly how long does a spacecraft in low Earth orbit, like the International Space Station, take to circle Earth once?","o":["About 10 minutes","About 12 hours","About 24 hours","About 90 minutes"],"a":3,"e":"That means astronauts on board see around 16 sunrises and sunsets every day."},
  {"cat":"space","q":"Which planet has the most extreme winds measured in the solar system, reaching over 2,000 km/h?","o":["Neptune","Earth","Mars","Mercury"],"a":0,"e":"These are the fastest winds measured in the solar system, remarkable for a planet that gets so little heat from the Sun."},
  {"cat":"animals","q":"What is the largest animal alive today?","o":["African elephant","Blue whale","Whale shark","Giant squid"],"a":1,"e":"A blue whale can grow to around 30 m long, longer than two buses parked end to end.","k":1},
  {"cat":"animals","q":"How many hearts does an octopus have?","o":["1","2","3","8"],"a":2,"e":"Its blood is blue, because it uses a copper-based molecule to carry oxygen.","k":1},
  {"cat":"animals","q":"Which Australian animal is the largest living carnivorous marsupial?","o":["Quoll","Numbat","Wombat","Tasmanian devil"],"a":3,"e":"Devils have one of the strongest bites for their size of any mammal and can crunch through bone.","k":1},
  {"cat":"animals","q":"In emus, which parent sits on the eggs until they hatch?","o":["The father","The mother","Both parents take turns","Neither, they bury the eggs"],"a":0,"e":"The male sits on the nest for about eight weeks, rarely eating or drinking."},
  {"cat":"animals","q":"What is a baby echidna called?","o":["A joey","A puggle","A pup","A kit"],"a":1,"e":"Echidnas lay eggs, and the puggle hatches inside its mother's pouch.","k":1},
  {"cat":"animals","q":"What word describes animals that are most active at dawn and dusk?","o":["Nocturnal","Diurnal","Crepuscular","Arboreal"],"a":2,"e":"Many kangaroos and wallabies are crepuscular, which is why drivers need extra care at those times."},
  {"cat":"animals","q":"Which is the only group of mammals capable of true flight?","o":["Flying squirrels","Sugar gliders","Flying lemurs","Bats"],"a":3,"e":"Gliders only glide, while bats flap their wings, and they make up about a fifth of all mammal species.","k":1},
  {"cat":"animals","q":"The thylacine, or Tasmanian tiger, was what kind of animal?","o":["Big cat","Marsupial","Wild dog","Hyena"],"a":1,"e":"Despite its stripes and dog-like looks, it carried its young in a pouch, and unusually the males had a pouch too.","k":1},
  {"cat":"animals","q":"What is a group of owls called?","o":["A murder","A parliament","A flock","A pride"],"a":1,"e":"The name is thought to come from owls' old reputation for wisdom."},
  {"cat":"animals","q":"Why do snakes flick their tongues in and out?","o":["To cool down","To hear sounds","To smell by collecting scent particles","To scare predators"],"a":2,"e":"The tongue delivers scents to a special organ in the roof of the mouth called the Jacobson's organ."},
  {"cat":"animals","q":"Which sea animal has no brain, heart or bones?","o":["Octopus","Crab","Seahorse","Jellyfish"],"a":3,"e":"Jellyfish have drifted through the oceans for more than 500 million years.","k":1},
  {"cat":"animals","q":"Which Australian bird is famous for mimicking sounds like chainsaws and camera shutters?","o":["Superb lyrebird","Laughing kookaburra","Sulphur-crested cockatoo","Australian magpie"],"a":0,"e":"The superb lyrebird appears on the Australian 10 cent coin.","k":1},
  {"cat":"animals","q":"What is the hard case a caterpillar forms before it becomes a butterfly?","o":["A cocoon of silk","A chrysalis","A burrow","A shell"],"a":1,"e":"Inside the chrysalis, the caterpillar's body breaks down and rebuilds itself into a butterfly.","k":1},
  {"cat":"animals","q":"Which animals are humans' closest living relatives?","o":["Gorillas","Orangutans","Chimpanzees and bonobos","Gibbons"],"a":2,"e":"Humans share about 98.8% of their DNA with chimpanzees."},
  {"cat":"animals","q":"In seahorses, which parent gives birth?","o":["The male","The female","Both together","Neither, they lay eggs on reefs"],"a":0,"e":"The female places her eggs in the male's pouch, and he carries them until the babies are born.","k":1},
  {"cat":"animals","q":"Sharks can sense the tiny electric fields of hidden prey using organs called what?","o":["Lateral lines","Ampullae of Lorenzini","Gill rakers","Swim bladders"],"a":1,"e":"These jelly-filled pores on the snout can detect incredibly faint electric signals from muscles."},
  {"cat":"animals","q":"Which land mammal has the longest pregnancy?","o":["Hippopotamus","Rhinoceros","African elephant","Giraffe"],"a":2,"e":"Elephant pregnancies last about 22 months, and a newborn calf can weigh around 100 kg."},
  {"cat":"animals","q":"The axolotl, a salamander from Mexico, is famous for which ability?","o":["Changing colour instantly","Breathing fire","Living without water","Regrowing lost body parts"],"a":3,"e":"It can regrow limbs and even parts of its heart and brain, and keeps its frilly gills as an adult."},
  {"cat":"animals","q":"Which bird reaches the highest speeds of any animal when diving for prey?","o":["Peregrine falcon","Wedge-tailed eagle","Albatross","Swift"],"a":0,"e":"In a steep dive, called a stoop, it can exceed 300 km/h."},
  {"cat":"animals","q":"Baleen, which some whales use to filter food, is made of what?","o":["Bone","Keratin","Cartilage","Enamel"],"a":1,"e":"Keratin is the same protein found in your hair and fingernails."},
  {"cat":"animals","q":"Which is the largest species of penguin?","o":["King penguin","Little penguin","Emperor penguin","Gentoo penguin"],"a":2,"e":"Male emperors huddle through the Antarctic winter, each balancing an egg on its feet.","k":1},
  {"cat":"animals","q":"What gives flamingos their pink colour?","o":["The sun bleaching their feathers","Their blood showing through","Mud they roll in","Pigments in the food they eat"],"a":3,"e":"Their diet of algae and small shrimp is rich in carotenoids, the same pigments that colour carrots.","k":1},
  {"cat":"animals","q":"Tardigrades, tiny animals about half a millimetre long, are famous for what?","o":["Surviving extreme conditions, even space","Being the fastest insects","Living for thousands of years","Glowing in the dark"],"a":0,"e":"Nicknamed 'water bears', they can dry out completely and revive years later when water returns."},
  {"cat":"animals","q":"How do honeybees tell other bees where to find food?","o":["By leaving a trail of wax","By doing a 'waggle dance'","By buzzing a tune","By changing colour"],"a":1,"e":"Karl von Frisch shared a Nobel Prize in 1973 partly for decoding what the dance means.","k":1},
  {"cat":"animals","q":"What is the largest living reptile?","o":["Komodo dragon","Green anaconda","Saltwater crocodile","Leatherback turtle"],"a":2,"e":"'Salties' live across northern Australia and Southeast Asia, and large males can exceed 6 m."},
  {"cat":"animals","q":"Scientists found that some dung beetles use what to steer at night?","o":["The Moon's craters","Earth's magnetic field only","Smells carried on the wind","The Milky Way"],"a":3,"e":"It was the first animal shown to navigate using the Milky Way."},
  {"cat":"body","q":"How many bones are in the adult human body?","o":["106","186","206","306"],"a":2,"e":"Babies are born with about 300 bones, and many fuse together as they grow.","k":1},
  {"cat": "body", "q": "Why do your fingertips go wrinkly after a long bath?", "o": ["Your skin simply soaks up water and swells", "Your nerves tell blood vessels to narrow, puckering the skin", "Your skin loses its natural oils", "The water dissolves the top layer of skin"], "a": 1, "e": "Fingers with damaged nerves don't wrinkle. Some studies suggest the wrinkles help us grip wet objects."},
  {"cat":"body","q":"What is the largest internal organ in the human body?","o":["Lungs","Brain","Liver","Heart"],"a":2,"e":"The liver can regrow to full size even after a large part of it has been removed."},
  {"cat":"body","q":"What is the largest muscle in the human body?","o":["Biceps","Quadriceps","Latissimus dorsi","Gluteus maximus"],"a":3,"e":"This buttock muscle helps keep you upright and powers you up stairs and hills."},
  {"cat":"body","q":"Which part of the brain mainly coordinates balance and smooth movement?","o":["Cerebellum","Cerebrum","Brainstem","Hypothalamus"],"a":0,"e":"Its name means 'little brain', yet it holds more than half of all the brain's nerve cells."},
  {"cat":"body","q":"How many teeth does a full set of adult teeth have, including wisdom teeth?","o":["20","28","32","36"],"a":2,"e":"Children have 20 baby teeth, which start falling out around age six.","k":1},
  {"cat":"body","q":"What is the coloured part of your eye called?","o":["Pupil","Retina","Iris","Lens"],"a":2,"e":"Iris patterns are so unique that your left and right eyes don't even match each other.","k":1},
  {"cat":"body","q":"Which vitamin does your skin make when exposed to sunlight?","o":["Vitamin C","Vitamin A","Vitamin B12","Vitamin D"],"a":3,"e":"Vitamin D helps your body absorb calcium to build strong bones.","k":1},
  {"cat":"body","q":"Which blood cells help fight infection?","o":["White blood cells","Red blood cells","Platelets","Skin cells"],"a":0,"e":"Your body makes billions of new white blood cells every day in the bone marrow.","k":1},
  {"cat":"body","q":"Which bone is the only one in the human body not joined to any other bone?","o":["Kneecap","Hyoid","Collarbone","Tailbone"],"a":1,"e":"This small U-shaped bone in the neck supports the tongue and helps you swallow."},
  {"cat":"body","q":"Which mineral sits at the centre of haemoglobin, helping it hold on to oxygen?","o":["Calcium","Zinc","Iron","Magnesium"],"a":2,"e":"Iron is also what makes blood red."},
  {"cat":"body","q":"What connects muscles to bones?","o":["Ligaments","Cartilage","Nerves","Tendons"],"a":3,"e":"Ligaments, by contrast, connect one bone to another.","k":1},
  {"cat":"body","q":"Roughly how long is the adult small intestine?","o":["Several metres","About 30 cm","About 1 m","About 50 m"],"a":0,"e":"It is folded tightly inside your belly, and its lining has a huge surface area to absorb food."},
  {"cat":"body","q":"What is the name for the body's natural roughly 24-hour cycle of sleeping and waking?","o":["Metabolic rate","Circadian rhythm","Biorhythm pulse","REM cycle"],"a":1,"e":"'Circadian' comes from Latin words meaning 'about a day'."},
  {"cat":"body","q":"Which pea-sized gland is often called the body's 'master gland'?","o":["Thyroid gland","Adrenal gland","Pituitary gland","Pineal gland"],"a":2,"e":"It sits at the base of the brain and sends out hormones that control many other glands."},
  {"cat":"body","q":"What does the snail-shaped cochlea in your inner ear do?","o":["Controls blinking","Helps you taste","Keeps your ear clean","Turns sound vibrations into nerve signals"],"a":3,"e":"Tiny hair cells inside it respond to different pitches, and once damaged they don't grow back."},
  {"cat":"body","q":"What is the common name for the patella?","o":["Kneecap","Shoulder blade","Collarbone","Shin bone"],"a":0,"e":"Babies' kneecaps are mostly soft cartilage, which slowly hardens into bone in early childhood.","k":1},
  {"cat":"body","q":"What is the main job of platelets in the blood?","o":["Carrying oxygen","Helping blood to clot","Fighting germs","Carrying hormones"],"a":1,"e":"Platelets are tiny fragments of cells, and each one lasts only about a week to ten days."},
  {"cat":"body","q":"Roughly how many of your 206 bones are in your hands and feet combined?","o":["About a tenth","About a quarter","More than half","Almost none"],"a":2,"e":"Each hand has 27 bones and each foot has 26, giving 106 in total."},
  {"cat":"body","q":"What causes goosebumps?","o":["Cold blood near the skin","Nerves swelling","Sweat glands closing","Tiny muscles pulling hairs upright"],"a":3,"e":"In our furry ancestors this puffed up the fur for warmth or to look bigger.","k":1},
  {"cat":"body","q":"What does the diaphragm help you do?","o":["Breathe","Digest food","See clearly","Walk"],"a":0,"e":"Hiccups happen when the diaphragm suddenly spasms and the vocal cords snap shut.","k":1},
  {"cat":"body","q":"Roughly how much blood does an average adult have?","o":["About 1 litre","About 5 litres","About 15 litres","About 50 litres"],"a":1,"e":"The heart pumps roughly that whole amount around the body about once every minute at rest."},
  {"cat":"body","q":"What is a typical normal body temperature for a healthy person?","o":["About 32°C","About 40°C","About 37°C","About 42°C"],"a":2,"e":"It naturally rises and falls a little through the day and is usually lowest in the early morning.","k":1},
  {"cat":"body","q":"What is the hardest substance in the human body?","o":["Thigh bone","Fingernails","Skull bone","Tooth enamel"],"a":3,"e":"The cells that make enamel are lost once a tooth comes through, so enamel can't regrow.","k":1},
  {"cat":"body","q":"What are the body's smallest blood vessels called?","o":["Capillaries","Arteries","Veins","Aortas"],"a":0,"e":"Their walls are just one cell thick, letting oxygen and nutrients pass through to tissues."},
  {"cat":"body","q":"Which small organ stores bile, which helps digest fat?","o":["Appendix","Gallbladder","Spleen","Bladder"],"a":1,"e":"Bile is made by the liver, and the gallbladder squeezes it into the gut after a fatty meal."},
  {"cat":"body","q":"When you hit your 'funny bone', what are you actually knocking?","o":["A bone","A tendon","A nerve","A blood vessel"],"a":2,"e":"The ulnar nerve runs close to the skin at the elbow, which is why a bump causes that tingling jolt.","k":1},
  {"cat":"body","q":"What type of joint is your shoulder?","o":["Hinge joint","Pivot joint","Gliding joint","Ball-and-socket joint"],"a":3,"e":"Its range of movement makes the shoulder the most mobile joint, but also one of the easiest to dislocate."},
  {"cat":"history","q":"In what year did Australia's six colonies federate to form the Commonwealth of Australia?","o":["1788","1851","1901","1914"],"a":2,"e":"Edmund Barton became the first Prime Minister, and Melbourne served as the temporary seat of government."},
  {"cat":"history","q":"The Eureka Stockade rebellion of 1854 took place on the goldfields near which town?","o":["Bendigo","Kalgoorlie","Ballarat","Bathurst"],"a":2,"e":"Miners were protesting against costly mining licences, and their blue and white Southern Cross flag is still a famous symbol."},
  {"cat":"animals","q":"Quokkas, famous for their 'smiling' photos, live mainly on which island?","o":["Kangaroo Island","Magnetic Island","Phillip Island","Rottnest Island"],"a":3,"e":"Dutch explorer Willem de Vlamingh thought they were giant rats, so he named the island 'rat nest'.","k":1},
  {"cat":"culture","q":"Which language has the most native speakers in the world?","o":["Mandarin Chinese","Spanish","Hindi","English"],"a":0,"e":"Mandarin has around 900 million native speakers, far more than any other language."},
  {"cat":"culture","q":"Diwali, celebrated by Hindus, Sikhs and Jains, is known as the festival of what?","o":["Lights","Kites","Colours","Harvest"],"a":0,"e":"Families light rows of small clay oil lamps called diyas to symbolise light winning over darkness.","k":1},
  {"cat":"culture","q":"Paella originally comes from which region of Spain?","o":["Andalusia","Galicia","Valencia","Catalonia"],"a":2,"e":"The original Valencian version used rabbit, chicken and beans rather than seafood."},
  {"cat":"culture","q":"Kimchi, a spicy fermented vegetable dish, is a staple food of which country?","o":["Korea","Thailand","Vietnam","Japan"],"a":0,"e":"Kimjang, the communal autumn making of kimchi, is on UNESCO's list of Intangible Cultural Heritage.","k":1},
  {"cat":"culture","q":"The Guru Granth Sahib is the holy scripture of which religion?","o":["Jainism","Hinduism","Buddhism","Sikhism"],"a":3,"e":"Sikhs treat it as a living Guru, and hymns from it are sung throughout the day at the Golden Temple in Amritsar."},
  {"cat":"culture","q":"During Ramadan, many Muslims fast between which times each day?","o":["Sunset and dawn","Midnight and noon","Sunrise and midday","Dawn and sunset"],"a":3,"e":"The fast is often broken at sunset with dates and water, in a meal called iftar."},
  {"cat":"culture","q":"The tango dance first developed in which part of the world?","o":["Brazil","Argentina and Uruguay","Cuba","Spain"],"a":1,"e":"It grew up in the late 1800s in the working-class neighbourhoods of Buenos Aires and Montevideo."},
  {"cat":"culture","q":"The Hindi-language film industry known as Bollywood is based mainly in which city?","o":["Kolkata","Delhi","Chennai","Mumbai"],"a":3,"e":"The name blends Bombay, the city's former name, with Hollywood."},
  {"cat":"culture","q":"What is traditionally mixed into sushi rice to give it a tangy flavour?","o":["Lemon juice","Miso","Soy sauce","Rice vinegar"],"a":3,"e":"Sushi began as a way of preserving fish in fermented rice, and vinegar later replaced the slow fermentation."},
  {"cat":"culture","q":"Oktoberfest is held every year in which city?","o":["Vienna","Munich","Berlin","Hamburg"],"a":1,"e":"It began in 1810 as a celebration of the wedding of Bavaria's Crown Prince Ludwig and Princess Therese.","k":1},
  {"cat":"culture","q":"What is Esperanto?","o":["A language invented to be easy to learn internationally","A form of sign language","An old dialect of Spanish","The ancient language of the Basques"],"a":0,"e":"It was published in 1887 by L. L. Zamenhof, who hoped a shared second language would help people get along."},
  {"cat":"culture","q":"The sitar is a stringed instrument closely linked with the classical music of which country?","o":["India","China","Turkey","Iran"],"a":0,"e":"Ravi Shankar made the sitar famous in the West, and taught George Harrison of the Beatles to play it."},
  {"cat":"culture","q":"Which 1927 film is famous as the first feature-length 'talkie' with synchronised dialogue?","o":["The Jazz Singer","Citizen Kane","The Wizard of Oz","Metropolis"],"a":0,"e":"Most of the film is actually silent with title cards, with only a few scenes of synchronised singing and speech."},
  {"cat":"culture","q":"Samba music and dance are most closely linked with the Carnival of which country?","o":["Mexico","Portugal","Brazil","Colombia"],"a":2,"e":"In Rio de Janeiro, samba schools compete in a purpose-built parade venue called the Sambadrome.","k":1},
  {"cat":"culture","q":"How many nights does the Jewish festival of Hanukkah last?","o":["Twelve","Seven","Eight","Three"],"a":2,"e":"The special Hanukkah candle holder has nine branches: one for each night plus a helper candle used to light the others.","k":1},
  {"cat":"culture","q":"Haggis is a traditional dish from which country?","o":["Scotland","Ireland","Iceland","Wales"],"a":0,"e":"It is served on Burns Night each January, when Robert Burns's poem 'Address to a Haggis' is recited."},
  {"cat":"culture","q":"Hangul is the alphabet used to write which language?","o":["Korean","Thai","Japanese","Mongolian"],"a":0,"e":"It was created in the 15th century under King Sejong the Great so that ordinary people could learn to read."},
  {"cat":"culture","q":"What is the traditional Japanese tea ceremony called?","o":["Chanoyu","Origami","Kabuki","Ikebana"],"a":0,"e":"The ceremony centres on matcha, a powdered green tea whisked with hot water in a bowl."},
  {"cat":"culture","q":"Flamenco music and dance are most associated with which region of Spain?","o":["Catalonia","Andalusia","Madrid","Basque Country"],"a":1,"e":"Flamenco blends influences from the region's Roma people with Spanish, Moorish and Jewish traditions."},
  {"cat":"culture","q":"Which composer wrote the set of violin concertos called The Four Seasons?","o":["Johann Sebastian Bach","George Frideric Handel","Antonio Vivaldi","Wolfgang Amadeus Mozart"],"a":2,"e":"Each concerto was published with a short poem describing the scenes the music paints, such as birdsong and storms.","k":1},
  {"cat":"culture","q":"Which composer wrote his Ninth Symphony while almost completely deaf?","o":["Ludwig van Beethoven","Franz Schubert","Johannes Brahms","Joseph Haydn"],"a":0,"e":"Its final movement, known as the 'Ode to Joy', is now used as the anthem of Europe.","k":1},
  {"cat":"culture","q":"The Cannes Film Festival is held each year in which country?","o":["France","Monaco","Spain","Italy"],"a":0,"e":"Its top prize is the Palme d'Or, meaning 'Golden Palm'."},
  {"cat":"culture","q":"What was Walt Disney's first full-length animated feature film?","o":["Snow White and the Seven Dwarfs","Cinderella","Pinocchio","Bambi"],"a":0,"e":"Released in 1937, it was nicknamed 'Disney's Folly' by doubters before becoming a huge hit.","k":1},
  {"cat":"culture","q":"Traditional Italian risotto is made with which grain?","o":["Oats","Barley","Rice","Wheat"],"a":2,"e":"Short-grain varieties such as Arborio release starch as they cook, giving risotto its creamy texture.","k":1},
  {"cat":"culture","q":"The French croissant is thought to have developed from the kipferl, a crescent-shaped pastry from which country?","o":["Switzerland","Hungary","Austria","Poland"],"a":2,"e":"This is why French bakers call croissants and similar pastries 'viennoiserie', meaning things from Vienna."},
  {"cat":"culture","q":"What does the Japanese word 'tsunami' literally mean?","o":["Storm tide","Moving sea","Great water","Harbour wave"],"a":3,"e":"Fishers returning home would find their harbours devastated by waves they had barely noticed out at sea."},
  {"cat":"culture","q":"Afrikaans, which developed from Dutch, is mainly spoken in which country?","o":["Kenya","Ghana","Nigeria","South Africa"],"a":3,"e":"It grew from the Dutch spoken by settlers at the Cape from the 1600s, mixed with other languages over time."},
  {"cat":"culture","q":"What is the Vietnamese Lunar New Year festival called?","o":["Songkran","Chuseok","Tết","Loy Krathong"],"a":2,"e":"Songkran is the Thai New Year, famous for its good-natured water fights."},
  {"cat":"culture","q":"The saxophone is made of brass, but which family of instruments does it belong to?","o":["Woodwind","Percussion","Brass","Strings"],"a":0,"e":"It counts as a woodwind because its sound comes from a reed, and it was invented by Adolphe Sax in the 1840s.","k":1},
  {"cat":"economics","q":"Who wrote The Wealth of Nations, published in 1776?","o":["John Maynard Keynes","Karl Marx","Adam Smith","David Hume"],"a":2,"e":"His famous phrase 'invisible hand' appears only once in the whole book."},
  {"cat":"economics","q":"What did John Maynard Keynes argue governments should do during a recession?","o":["Cut spending to balance the budget","Raise taxes sharply","Leave markets entirely alone","Increase spending to boost demand"],"a":3,"e":"His 1936 book The General Theory shaped how many governments responded to downturns for decades."},
  {"cat": "economics", "q": "What is a 'Veblen good'?", "o": ["A product people want more of as its price rises, because it signals status", "A basic item people buy no matter the price", "A product that gets cheaper when made in bulk", "A service the government provides for free"], "a": 0, "e": "It's named after economist Thorstein Veblen, who coined 'conspicuous consumption'. Luxury handbags and watches are classic examples."},
  {"cat":"economics","q":"What is a monopoly?","o":["A market with only one seller","A group of countries that trade freely","A tax on imported goods","A market with only one buyer"],"a":0,"e":"The board game Monopoly grew out of The Landlord's Game, designed to show the problems of concentrated land ownership.","k":1},
  {"cat":"economics","q":"What does 'CPI', the most common measure of inflation, stand for?","o":["Cost of Production Index","Central Pricing Indicator","Consumer Price Index","Consumer Purchasing Income"],"a":2,"e":"In Australia, the Australian Bureau of Statistics calculates CPI by tracking the prices of a basket of everyday goods and services."},
  {"cat":"economics","q":"Which country was the first to use paper money widely?","o":["England","China","Italy","Egypt"],"a":1,"e":"Paper money spread in China around 1,000 years ago, and Marco Polo later marvelled at it in his travel accounts.","k":1},
  {"cat":"economics","q":"What is compound interest?","o":["Interest that goes down over time","A fixed fee paid once a year","Interest charged only on late payments","Interest earned on both your original money and past interest"],"a":3,"e":"Over long periods compounding snowballs, which is why starting to save early makes such a difference."},
  {"cat":"economics","q":"The 'tulip mania' of the 1630s took place in which country?","o":["England","France","Turkey","The Netherlands"],"a":3,"e":"Some rare bulbs reportedly sold for more than a house, though historians now think its wider damage was exaggerated."},
  {"cat": "economics", "q": "What does it mean when a government runs a budget deficit?", "o": ["It spends more than it collects in a year", "It collects more than it spends in a year", "It has paid off all its debts", "It has stopped collecting taxes"], "a": 0, "e": "The gap is usually covered by borrowing, which adds to government debt. The opposite is a budget surplus.", "k": 1},
  {"cat":"economics","q":"In finance, what does 'IPO' stand for?","o":["Internal Payment Operation","International Price Order","Investment Profit Option","Initial Public Offering"],"a":3,"e":"An IPO is when a company first sells its shares to the public on a stock exchange."},
  {"cat":"economics","q":"The idea that 'bad money drives out good' is named after which person?","o":["Jean-Baptiste Say","Thomas Malthus","David Ricardo","Thomas Gresham"],"a":3,"e":"Gresham was a 16th-century English financier, and his 'law' explains why people hoard coins with more precious metal in them."},
  {"cat":"economics","q":"David Ricardo is famous for which idea about trade?","o":["Comparative advantage","The invisible hand","Supply-side economics","The multiplier effect"],"a":0,"e":"His classic example showed England and Portugal both gaining by specialising in cloth and wine."},
  {"cat":"economics","q":"What did Thomas Malthus famously warn about in 1798?","o":["Governments printing too much money","Machines replacing all workers","Banks lending too much money","Population growth outpacing food supply"],"a":3,"e":"Farming productivity rose faster than he expected, but his ideas influenced Charles Darwin's thinking on natural selection."},
  {"cat":"economics","q":"Which two institutions were created at the 1944 Bretton Woods conference?","o":["The WTO and the United Nations","The IMF and the World Bank","The European Central Bank and the IMF","The OECD and the G20"],"a":1,"e":"The conference was held at a hotel in New Hampshire, with delegates from 44 Allied nations."},
  {"cat":"economics","q":"A 'technical recession' is commonly defined as what?","o":["Two quarters in a row of shrinking GDP","A fall in share prices of 20%","One month of rising unemployment","A year of high inflation"],"a":0,"e":"It's a rule of thumb, and economists often look at broader measures such as jobs and spending too."},
  {"cat":"economics","q":"The Wall Street Crash that helped trigger the Great Depression happened in which year?","o":["1939","1929","1945","1914"],"a":1,"e":"The worst days came in late October, remembered as Black Thursday and Black Tuesday."},
  {"cat": "economics", "q": "What is a 'monopsony'?", "o": ["A market with only one seller", "A market with only one buyer", "A tax on luxury goods", "A market with no rules"], "a": 1, "e": "A single big employer in a small town can act like one, because workers have few other places to sell their labour."},
  {"cat":"economics","q":"Which currency uses the symbol £?","o":["The Indian rupee","The Swiss franc","The euro","The British pound"],"a":3,"e":"The £ sign comes from a fancy letter L, standing for 'libra', a Roman unit of weight.","k":1},
  {"cat":"economics","q":"When Australia switched to decimal currency in 1966, which currency did the dollar replace?","o":["The Australian crown","The sovereign","The Australian pound","The British shilling"],"a":2,"e":"The changeover was promoted with a catchy television jingle featuring a cartoon character called Dollar Bill."},
  {"cat":"economics","q":"In 1988, Australia became the first country to issue banknotes made from what?","o":["Cotton","Hemp","Silk","Polymer (plastic)"],"a":3,"e":"The first was a $10 note marking the bicentenary, and polymer notes last much longer than paper ones.","k":1},
  {"cat":"economics","q":"Which country printed a 100 trillion dollar banknote during severe hyperinflation in the late 2000s?","o":["Zimbabwe","Germany","Hungary","Venezuela"],"a":0,"e":"The worst hyperinflation ever recorded was in Hungary in 1946, when prices roughly tripled every day."},
  {"cat":"economics","q":"What is barter?","o":["Selling shares on the stock market","Paying for things by credit card","Swapping goods or services without using money","Borrowing money from a bank"],"a":2,"e":"Barter still happens today, for example when neighbours swap garden produce or skills.","k":1},
  {"cat":"economics","q":"What does the law of 'diminishing marginal utility' describe?","o":["Workers become less productive with age","Each extra unit of something brings less satisfaction","Prices always fall over time","Costs rise as a business grows"],"a":1,"e":"Your first slice of pizza usually feels much more rewarding than your fifth."},
  {"cat":"economics","q":"What does 'GDP per capita' measure?","o":["The income of the average worker after tax","The total wealth of a country's richest people","The amount a government spends per person on health","A country's economic output divided by its population"],"a":3,"e":"It's often used to compare living standards, though it says nothing about how income is shared."},
  {"cat":"economics","q":"Which international organisation, founded in 1995, sets trade rules and settles trade disputes between countries?","o":["The United Nations Security Council","The World Trade Organization","The World Bank","The International Monetary Fund"],"a":1,"e":"It replaced an earlier agreement called GATT, which had governed world trade since 1948."},
  {"cat":"economics","q":"What is the Gini coefficient used to measure?","o":["Inflation","Income inequality","Economic growth","Unemployment"],"a":1,"e":"A score of 0 means everyone has the same income, while a score of 1 means one person has it all."},
  {"cat":"economics","q":"Which economist popularised the term 'creative destruction'?","o":["Adam Smith","Milton Friedman","Joseph Schumpeter","Friedrich Hayek"],"a":2,"e":"He described how new innovations sweep away old industries, as streaming did to video rental shops."},
  {"cat":"economics","q":"Milton Friedman is best known for which school of economic thought?","o":["Monetarism","Keynesianism","Marxism","Mercantilism"],"a":0,"e":"Monetarists argue that controlling the money supply is the key to keeping inflation in check."},
  {"cat":"australia","q":"Who was Australia's first Prime Minister?","o":["Henry Parkes","Edmund Barton","Alfred Deakin","John Curtin"],"a":1,"e":"After leaving politics in 1903, Barton became one of the first judges of the High Court of Australia."},
  {"cat":"australia","q":"The Great Barrier Reef lies off the coast of which state?","o":["Western Australia","New South Wales","Queensland","Northern Territory"],"a":2,"e":"Stretching more than 2,000 km, it's the largest structure on Earth built by living things.","k":1},
  {"cat":"australia","q":"In 1902, Australian women won which rights in federal elections?","o":["Only the right to vote","The right to vote and to stand for parliament","Only the right to stand for parliament","The right to vote in local councils only"],"a":1,"e":"Most Aboriginal people were excluded, and all Indigenous Australians only gained the federal vote in 1962."},
  {"cat":"australia","q":"The 1967 referendum, passed with over 90% of the vote, changed the Constitution so that Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander people would be...","o":["Counted in the national census","Given the right to vote for the first time","Represented by a separate parliament","Exempt from federal taxes"],"a":0,"e":"It remains one of only eight referendums ever passed out of the dozens put to Australians."},
  {"cat":"australia","q":"The High Court's 1992 Mabo decision overturned which legal idea?","o":["Common law","The White Australia policy","Terra nullius","Habeas corpus"],"a":2,"e":"Eddie Mabo came from Mer (Murray Island) in the Torres Strait, and the case recognised native title for the first time."},
  {"cat":"australia","q":"Which two animals appear on Australia's coat of arms?","o":["Platypus and echidna","Kangaroo and koala","Koala and kookaburra","Kangaroo and emu"],"a":3,"e":"The shield between them carries the badges of the six states.","k":1},
  {"cat":"australia","q":"Which body of water separates Tasmania from mainland Australia?","o":["Bass Strait","Tasman Sea","Torres Strait","Spencer Gulf"],"a":0,"e":"George Bass and Matthew Flinders sailed around Tasmania in 1798 and 1799, proving it was an island.","k":1},
  {"cat":"australia","q":"Which explorers made the first recorded European crossing of the Blue Mountains in 1813?","o":["Hume and Hovell","Blaxland, Lawson and Wentworth","Burke and Wills","Sturt and Mitchell"],"a":1,"e":"Earlier attempts had failed by following the valleys, while this group succeeded by following the ridges."},
  {"cat":"australia","q":"What were Burke and Wills attempting on their ill-fated 1860 to 1861 expedition?","o":["To cross Australia from south to north","To find gold in Western Australia","To sail around Australia","To climb Mount Kosciuszko"],"a":0,"e":"They reached the tidal mangroves near the Gulf of Carpentaria, but both men died on the return journey."},
  {"cat":"australia","q":"What is the upper house of Australia's federal parliament called?","o":["The Legislative Assembly","The House of Lords","The House of Representatives","The Senate"],"a":3,"e":"Each state elects the same number of senators, no matter its population."},
  {"cat":"australia","q":"What is the maximum term of the federal House of Representatives in Australia?","o":["Three years","Five years","Two years","Four years"],"a":0,"e":"It's one of the shortest terms among the world's democracies, while every state now has four-year terms."},
  {"cat":"australia","q":"Which Australian city was largely destroyed by Cyclone Tracy on Christmas Day 1974?","o":["Broome","Cairns","Darwin","Townsville"],"a":2,"e":"Most of the city's homes were destroyed and most residents were evacuated, leading to much stronger building codes."},
  {"cat": "australia", "q": "Which is Australia's smallest state?", "o": ["Victoria", "Tasmania", "South Australia", "New South Wales"], "a": 1, "e": "Victoria is the smallest mainland state. The ACT is smaller still, but it's a territory rather than a state.", "k": 1},
  {"cat":"australia","q":"Which Australian bird is famous for its loud 'laughing' call?","o":["Galah","Kookaburra","Lyrebird","Magpie"],"a":1,"e":"The laughing kookaburra is the largest member of the kingfisher family.","k":1},
  {"cat":"australia","q":"What was the main purpose of the Snowy Mountains Scheme, built between 1949 and 1974?","o":["To generate hydro-electricity and supply water for irrigation","To build a railway to Canberra","To mine coal in the Alps","To create ski resorts"],"a":0,"e":"More than 100,000 people from over 30 countries worked on it, many of them post-war migrants."},
  {"cat":"australia","q":"Who commanded the First Fleet, which arrived at Sydney Cove in 1788?","o":["Lachlan Macquarie","Captain James Cook","Captain Arthur Phillip","Matthew Flinders"],"a":2,"e":"Phillip became the first Governor of New South Wales, and 26 January, when he raised the British flag at Sydney Cove, is now Australia Day."},
  {"cat":"australia","q":"What are the three colours of the Aboriginal flag?","o":["Red, white and blue","Green, white and blue","Black, white and green","Black, red and yellow"],"a":3,"e":"Designed by Harold Thomas, it represents the people, the red earth and the sun, and was first flown in Adelaide in 1971.","k":1},
  {"cat":"australia","q":"Kakadu National Park, famous for ancient rock art, is in which state or territory?","o":["South Australia","Northern Territory","Queensland","Western Australia"],"a":1,"e":"It's World Heritage listed for both its natural and cultural values, a rare combination."},
  {"cat":"australia","q":"What is Australia's national anthem?","o":["Advance Australia Fair","I Still Call Australia Home","Waltzing Matilda","Song of Australia"],"a":0,"e":"It became the anthem in 1984, and in 2021 the line 'young and free' was changed to 'one and free'.","k":1},
  {"cat":"sport","q":"Which country hosted the first modern Olympic Games in 1896?","o":["England","Italy","Greece","France"],"a":2,"e":"The Games were held in Athens, as a nod to the ancient Olympics held in Greece.","k":1},
  {"cat":"sport","q":"How many rings are on the Olympic flag?","o":["Seven","Six","Four","Five"],"a":3,"e":"Pierre de Coubertin chose the colours because every nation's flag contained at least one of them, with the white background.","k":1},
  {"cat":"sport","q":"Which country hosted and won the first FIFA World Cup in 1930?","o":["Italy","Brazil","Argentina","Uruguay"],"a":3,"e":"Only 13 teams took part, and most European teams stayed away because of the long sea voyage."},
  {"cat":"sport","q":"Which tennis Grand Slam tournament is played on clay courts?","o":["The US Open","The French Open","Wimbledon","The Australian Open"],"a":1,"e":"Its home, Roland-Garros, is named after a French aviator who died in the First World War."},
  {"cat":"sport","q":"Which golf course in Scotland is known as 'the home of golf'?","o":["Augusta","St Andrews","Pebble Beach","Carnoustie"],"a":1,"e":"Golf has been played on the links at St Andrews for about 600 years."},
  {"cat":"sport","q":"In cricket, what does 'LBW' stand for?","o":["Last batter waiting","Leg before wicket","Low bowling width","Long ball wide"],"a":1,"e":"A batter can be given out LBW if the ball hits their leg when it would otherwise have hit the stumps.","k":1},
  {"cat":"sport","q":"How many players are on the field for each team in rugby union?","o":["15","13","18","11"],"a":0,"e":"Rugby league, which split from rugby union in 1895, uses 13 players per side."},
  {"cat":"sport","q":"In which sport do players talk about the 'hammer', the 'house' and 'sweeping'?","o":["Curling","Croquet","Bowls","Ice hockey"],"a":0,"e":"Many curling stones are made from granite quarried on Ailsa Craig, a small island off Scotland."},
  {"cat":"sport","q":"Basketball was invented in 1891 by James Naismith, who came from which country?","o":["Scotland","Canada","England","The United States"],"a":1,"e":"He invented the game in Springfield, Massachusetts, using peach baskets as the first hoops."},
  {"cat":"sport","q":"How many points is a touchdown worth in American football?","o":["Six","Five","Three","Seven"],"a":0,"e":"After a touchdown, the team can try for one or two extra points.","k":1},
  {"cat":"sport","q":"What is the maximum possible break in a standard game of snooker?","o":["180","147","150","100"],"a":1,"e":"It requires potting all 15 reds, each followed by the black, then all six colours in order."},
  {"cat":"sport","q":"The Stanley Cup is awarded in which sport?","o":["Rowing","Ice hockey","Baseball","Lacrosse"],"a":1,"e":"It was donated by Lord Stanley, a Governor General of Canada, and first awarded in 1893."},
  {"cat":"sport","q":"In the Tour de France, what colour jersey does the overall leader wear?","o":["Green","Red","White","Yellow"],"a":3,"e":"Yellow was the colour of the paper of L'Auto, the newspaper that founded the race in 1903.","k":1},
  {"cat":"sport","q":"Sumo wrestling is the traditional national sport of which country?","o":["China","Japan","Korea","Mongolia"],"a":1,"e":"The highest rank a sumo wrestler can reach is yokozuna, meaning grand champion.","k":1},
  {"cat":"sport","q":"How long is an Olympic swimming pool?","o":["100 metres","25 metres","75 metres","50 metres"],"a":3,"e":"Olympic pools also have ten lanes, though only the middle eight are used in races.","k":1},
  {"cat":"sport","q":"How many points is a try worth in rugby union?","o":["Three","Five","Four","Six"],"a":1,"e":"In early rugby a try scored no points at all, it only earned the chance to 'try' a kick at goal."},
  {"cat":"sport","q":"In Australian rules football, how many points is a goal worth?","o":["Three","Six","One","Seven"],"a":1,"e":"A behind is worth one point, and a match score such as 12.8 (80) shows goals, behinds and total.","k":1},
  {"cat":"sport","q":"The America's Cup is a famous trophy in which sport?","o":["Rowing","Golf","Sailing","Horse racing"],"a":2,"e":"Australia II won it in 1983, ending the longest winning streak in sporting history, which dated from 1851."},
  {"cat":"sport","q":"Which martial art's name means 'the gentle way'?","o":["Kung fu","Karate","Judo","Taekwondo"],"a":2,"e":"Judo was founded by Jigoro Kano in 1882 and became an Olympic sport at the 1964 Tokyo Games."},
  {"cat":"sport","q":"How long is one lap of a standard outdoor running track, measured in the inside lane?","o":["200 metres","400 metres","1,000 metres","500 metres"],"a":1,"e":"Runners in outer lanes start further ahead in staggered positions so everyone runs the same distance.","k":1},
  {"cat":"sport","q":"How many events make up the men's Olympic decathlon?","o":["Twelve","Ten","Five","Seven"],"a":1,"e":"The events are held over two days, while the women's heptathlon has seven events."},
  {"cat":"sport","q":"The sport of badminton takes its name from what?","o":["A British army officer","A town in India","A country house in England","A type of feather"],"a":2,"e":"It became popular at Badminton House, the Duke of Beaufort's estate in Gloucestershire, in the 1800s."},
  {"cat":"sport","q":"In baseball, how many strikes does it take for a batter to be out?","o":["Five","Two","Four","Three"],"a":3,"e":"Four balls outside the strike zone, on the other hand, let the batter walk to first base.","k":1},
  {"cat":"sport","q":"In which year were the first Wimbledon tennis championships held?","o":["1924","1901","1877","1851"],"a":2,"e":"Only men's singles was played that first year, with 22 players entering."},
  {"cat":"sport","q":"What is the Latin Olympic motto 'Citius, Altius, Fortius' in English?","o":["Honour, Glory, Victory","Unity, Peace, Sport","Bigger, Better, Bolder","Faster, Higher, Stronger"],"a":3,"e":"In 2021 the word 'Communiter', meaning 'together', was added to the motto."},
  {"cat":"sport","q":"The 'Fosbury Flop' technique is used in which athletics event?","o":["Pole vault","Long jump","High jump","Triple jump"],"a":2,"e":"Dick Fosbury won gold with his backwards technique at the 1968 Mexico City Olympics, and it soon became standard."},
  {"cat":"sport","q":"Kabaddi, a team contact sport, is most popular in which region?","o":["West Africa","Scandinavia","South Asia","South America"],"a":2,"e":"It has been part of the Asian Games since 1990."},
  {"cat":"art","q":"Who painted The Starry Night?","o":["Paul Gauguin","Paul Cézanne","Vincent van Gogh","Claude Monet"],"a":2,"e":"He painted it in 1889 from memory of the view from his window at an asylum in Saint-Rémy, France.","k":1},
  {"cat":"art","q":"Who painted the ceiling of the Sistine Chapel in the Vatican?","o":["Leonardo da Vinci","Donatello","Raphael","Michelangelo"],"a":3,"e":"He worked on it for about four years, from 1508 to 1512, mostly standing on scaffolding.","k":1},
  {"cat":"art","q":"Which French artist painted a famous series of water lilies from his garden?","o":["Claude Monet","Edgar Degas","Pierre-Auguste Renoir","Henri Matisse"],"a":0,"e":"He built a water garden at his home in Giverny and painted it about 250 times."},
  {"cat":"art","q":"The Impressionist movement took its name from a painting by which artist?","o":["Camille Pissarro","Berthe Morisot","Édouard Manet","Claude Monet"],"a":3,"e":"A critic mocked Monet's 'Impression, Sunrise', and the artists happily adopted the name."},
  {"cat":"art","q":"Artist Frida Kahlo came from which country?","o":["Cuba","Mexico","Argentina","Spain"],"a":1,"e":"Many of her best-known works are self-portraits, often painted while recovering from injuries.","k":1},
  {"cat":"art","q":"Which sculptor created The Thinker?","o":["Auguste Rodin","Michelangelo","Henry Moore","Constantin Brâncuși"],"a":0,"e":"It was first designed as part of a huge doorway, The Gates of Hell, to represent the poet Dante."},
  {"cat":"art","q":"The Parthenon in Athens was built as a temple to which goddess?","o":["Aphrodite","Artemis","Hera","Athena"],"a":3,"e":"Its columns are slightly curved and tilted so that they look perfectly straight from a distance.","k":1},
  {"cat":"art","q":"Which architect designed Fallingwater, a house built over a waterfall in Pennsylvania?","o":["Frank Gehry","Ludwig Mies van der Rohe","Le Corbusier","Frank Lloyd Wright"],"a":3,"e":"Wright believed buildings should be in harmony with their surroundings, an idea he called organic architecture."},
  {"cat":"art","q":"Which architect designed the titanium-clad Guggenheim Museum in Bilbao, Spain?","o":["Frank Gehry","Renzo Piano","Zaha Hadid","I. M. Pei"],"a":0,"e":"Its success in drawing visitors to the city led to the term 'the Bilbao effect'."},
  {"cat":"art","q":"Andy Warhol is a leading figure of which art movement?","o":["Cubism","Impressionism","Baroque","Pop Art"],"a":3,"e":"His 1962 work Campbell's Soup Cans shows 32 canvases, one for each soup flavour then on sale."},
  {"cat":"art","q":"In which country was the Bauhaus school of art and design founded in 1919?","o":["Austria","The Netherlands","France","Germany"],"a":3,"e":"Founded by architect Walter Gropius, it championed simple, functional design that still shapes furniture and buildings today."},
  {"cat":"art","q":"Which architect designed the Sagrada Família basilica in Barcelona?","o":["Antoni Gaudí","Santiago Calatrava","Joan Miró","Ricardo Bofill"],"a":0,"e":"Construction began in 1882, and Gaudí worked on it for over 40 years until his death in 1926."},
  {"cat":"art","q":"Picasso's painting Guernica was a response to what event?","o":["The bombing of a Basque town during the Spanish Civil War","A great flood in Barcelona","The sinking of the Titanic","The end of the First World War"],"a":0,"e":"The huge mural is painted only in black, white and grey, adding to its stark power."},
  {"cat":"art","q":"What is a fresco?","o":["A sculpture carved from wood","A picture made of small tiles","A print made from a metal plate","A painting made on fresh, wet plaster"],"a":3,"e":"As the plaster dries, the paint becomes part of the wall itself, which is why frescoes last for centuries."},
  {"cat":"art","q":"In art, what are 'complementary colours'?","o":["Colours next to each other on the colour wheel","Different shades of the same colour","Colours opposite each other on the colour wheel","Colours made by mixing black and white"],"a":2,"e":"Placed side by side, complementary colours like red and green make each other look brighter."},
  {"cat":"art","q":"Which Japanese artist made the woodblock print The Great Wave off Kanagawa?","o":["Kitagawa Utamaro","Utagawa Hiroshige","Katsushika Hokusai","Yayoi Kusama"],"a":2,"e":"It's part of a series called Thirty-six Views of Mount Fuji, and the mountain appears small in the background."},
  {"cat":"art","q":"Which Renaissance artist painted The School of Athens in the Vatican?","o":["Botticelli","Caravaggio","Titian","Raphael"],"a":3,"e":"Many believe the figure of Plato at its centre was modelled on Leonardo da Vinci."},
  {"cat":"art","q":"The Taj Mahal in India was built by Emperor Shah Jahan as a tomb for whom?","o":["His wife, Mumtaz Mahal","His favourite general","His mother","His father"],"a":0,"e":"It took about 20 years to complete and is made mainly of white marble inlaid with precious stones.","k":1},
  {"cat":"art","q":"Gustav Klimt's famous painting The Kiss uses a lot of which material?","o":["Silver paint","Copper wire","Gold leaf","Crushed glass"],"a":2,"e":"It comes from what is known as his 'Golden Period', when he often used gold leaf in his works."},
  {"cat":"art","q":"The Western Desert art movement, famous for dot painting, began in 1971 at which community?","o":["Hermannsburg","Maningrida","Papunya","Yuendumu"],"a":2,"e":"Teacher Geoffrey Bardon encouraged senior men to paint a mural on the school wall, sparking a world-famous art movement."},
  {"cat":"art","q":"Which Australian artist painted Shearing the Rams in 1890?","o":["Hans Heysen","Frederick McCubbin","Tom Roberts","Arthur Streeton"],"a":2,"e":"Roberts was a leading figure of the Heidelberg School, which captured Australian light and bush life."},
  {"cat":"art","q":"Which Dutch artist is known for grids of black lines filled with blocks of red, yellow and blue?","o":["Vincent van Gogh","Rembrandt","Piet Mondrian","M. C. Escher"],"a":2,"e":"He was a founder of the De Stijl movement, whose style later influenced fashion, graphic design and architecture."},
  {"cat":"art","q":"What material is Michelangelo's statue of David carved from?","o":["Marble","Wood","Bronze","Granite"],"a":0,"e":"It was carved from a single block that other sculptors had abandoned, and stands more than 5 metres tall.","k":1},
  {"cat":"art","q":"Which art style from about 1890 to 1910 is known for flowing, plant-like curves?","o":["Art Nouveau","Art Deco","Brutalism","Baroque"],"a":0,"e":"Hector Guimard's curly green entrances to the Paris Métro are classic examples."},
  {"cat":"art","q":"The Eiffel Tower was built for which event in 1889?","o":["The Olympic Games","A royal coronation","A World's Fair","The end of a war"],"a":2,"e":"It was meant to stand for only 20 years, and many artists at the time protested it was ugly.","k":1},
  {"cat":"art","q":"What does a 'still life' painting usually show?","o":["Scenes from famous battles","Wide views of the countryside","Objects such as fruit, flowers and bowls","People in motion"],"a":2,"e":"Dutch painters of the 1600s made still life hugely popular, often including symbols of how short life is.","k":1},
  {"cat":"art","q":"Brutalist architecture is best known for using which material?","o":["Red brick","Polished marble","Glass and steel","Raw concrete"],"a":3,"e":"The name comes from the French 'béton brut', meaning raw concrete, not from the word brutal."},
  {"cat":"art","q":"Which artist painted American Gothic, showing a farmer holding a pitchfork?","o":["Grant Wood","Andrew Wyeth","Edward Hopper","Norman Rockwell"],"a":0,"e":"The models were the artist's sister and his dentist."},
  {"cat":"literature","q":"Who wrote Pride and Prejudice?","o":["Emily Brontë","Jane Austen","Charlotte Brontë","George Eliot"],"a":1,"e":"Austen's early draft of the novel was titled First Impressions."},
  {"cat":"literature","q":"Who wrote Don Quixote, often called the first modern novel?","o":["Federico García Lorca","Lope de Vega","Miguel de Cervantes","Gabriel García Márquez"],"a":2,"e":"The phrase 'tilting at windmills' comes from the hero attacking windmills he believes are giants."},
  {"cat":"literature","q":"In Greek myth, who flew too close to the sun on wings of feathers and wax?","o":["Theseus","Perseus","Hermes","Icarus"],"a":3,"e":"The wings were made by his father Daedalus, who warned him to fly neither too high nor too low.","k":1},
  {"cat":"literature","q":"In Homer's Odyssey, which island is Odysseus trying to get home to?","o":["Sicily","Ithaca","Crete","Troy"],"a":1,"e":"His journey home after the Trojan War takes ten years, and his faithful old dog Argos recognises him despite his disguise."},
  {"cat":"literature","q":"Who wrote The Hobbit?","o":["Terry Pratchett","Roald Dahl","C. S. Lewis","J. R. R. Tolkien"],"a":3,"e":"Tolkien was an Oxford professor of language, and he invented whole languages for his Middle-earth stories.","k":1},
  {"cat":"literature","q":"Who is the Norse god of thunder?","o":["Freyr","Loki","Odin","Thor"],"a":3,"e":"The day Thursday is named after him, meaning 'Thor's day'.","k":1},
  {"cat":"literature","q":"Which poet wrote 'The Raven', with its famous refrain 'Nevermore'?","o":["Edgar Allan Poe","Robert Frost","Emily Dickinson","Walt Whitman"],"a":0,"e":"Poe was paid only a small sum for the poem, even though it made him famous almost overnight."},
  {"cat":"literature","q":"Lewis Carroll, author of Alice's Adventures in Wonderland, had what other job?","o":["Ship's captain","Mathematics lecturer","Doctor","Newspaper editor"],"a":1,"e":"His real name was Charles Dodgson, and he taught mathematics at Oxford University.","k":1},
  {"cat":"literature","q":"What is a haiku?","o":["A Chinese play","A long Greek story poem","A Persian love song","A short Japanese poem with three lines"],"a":3,"e":"A classic haiku has five, seven and five syllables, and often mentions a season.","k":1},
  {"cat":"literature","q":"The Epic of Gilgamesh, one of the oldest surviving works of literature, comes from which ancient civilisation?","o":["Ancient Greece","Mesopotamia","The Indus Valley","Ancient Egypt"],"a":1,"e":"It was written on clay tablets, and includes a great flood story with similarities to Noah's ark."},
  {"cat":"literature","q":"Who wrote One Hundred Years of Solitude?","o":["Isabel Allende","Gabriel García Márquez","Pablo Neruda","Jorge Luis Borges"],"a":1,"e":"It's set in the fictional town of Macondo and is a landmark of the style called magical realism."},
  {"cat":"literature","q":"Which Russian author wrote Crime and Punishment?","o":["Ivan Turgenev","Anton Chekhov","Leo Tolstoy","Fyodor Dostoevsky"],"a":3,"e":"Dostoevsky once faced a mock execution by firing squad before being sent to a Siberian prison camp instead."},
  {"cat":"literature","q":"Who created the detective Sherlock Holmes?","o":["Charles Dickens","Wilkie Collins","Agatha Christie","Arthur Conan Doyle"],"a":3,"e":"Doyle was a doctor, and he based Holmes's sharp powers of observation partly on his teacher Dr Joseph Bell.","k":1},
  {"cat":"literature","q":"Who wrote the words to 'Waltzing Matilda'?","o":["Henry Lawson","Dorothea Mackellar","Banjo Paterson","C. J. Dennis"],"a":2,"e":"He wrote it in 1895 while staying at Dagworth Station in outback Queensland.","k":1},
  {"cat":"literature","q":"Who wrote the Australian children's book Possum Magic?","o":["Jeannie Baker","Mem Fox","May Gibbs","Graeme Base"],"a":1,"e":"Grandma Poss helps invisible Hush become visible again by eating Australian foods like lamingtons and pavlova.","k":1},
  {"cat":"literature","q":"Dante wrote The Divine Comedy in Italian rather than which language most scholars then used?","o":["Greek","Spanish","Latin","French"],"a":2,"e":"Choosing everyday Tuscan helped make it the basis of the modern Italian language."},
  {"cat":"literature","q":"Who wrote The Tale of Genji, often called one of the world's first novels?","o":["Matsuo Bashō","Murasaki Shikibu","Sei Shōnagon","Confucius"],"a":1,"e":"She was a lady-in-waiting at the Japanese imperial court around the year 1000."},
  {"cat":"literature","q":"Which Shakespeare character says 'To be, or not to be, that is the question'?","o":["Macbeth","Othello","Hamlet","King Lear"],"a":2,"e":"Hamlet is Shakespeare's longest play, and uncut performances run for about four hours."},
  {"cat":"literature","q":"In Greek myth, what happened when Pandora opened her container?","o":["Her wishes all came true","Troubles and evils escaped into the world","The sun disappeared","The gods lost their powers"],"a":1,"e":"In the original myth it was a large jar, not a box, and only hope remained inside."},
  {"cat":"literature","q":"Who wrote The Great Gatsby?","o":["John Steinbeck","F. Scott Fitzgerald","William Faulkner","Ernest Hemingway"],"a":1,"e":"The green light at the end of Daisy's dock has become one of literature's best-known symbols of longing."},
  {"cat":"literature","q":"Who wrote the 1958 novel Things Fall Apart?","o":["Chinua Achebe","Ngũgĩ wa Thiong'o","Chimamanda Ngozi Adichie","Wole Soyinka"],"a":0,"e":"The title comes from a line in W. B. Yeats's poem 'The Second Coming'."},
  {"cat":"literature","q":"Who wrote The Chronicles of Narnia?","o":["J. R. R. Tolkien","C. S. Lewis","Enid Blyton","Roald Dahl"],"a":1,"e":"Lewis and Tolkien were close friends and belonged to an Oxford writers' group called the Inklings.","k":1},
  {"cat":"literature","q":"Which author created Peter Rabbit?","o":["Enid Blyton","Kenneth Grahame","Beatrix Potter","A. A. Milne"],"a":2,"e":"After publishers turned it down, she paid to print the first copies of The Tale of Peter Rabbit herself in 1901.","k":1},
  {"cat":"literature","q":"In Norse mythology, what is Yggdrasil?","o":["A giant tree linking the nine worlds","The hall of fallen warriors","Thor's hammer","Odin's eight-legged horse"],"a":0,"e":"Odin's horse is Sleipnir, Thor's hammer is Mjölnir and the warriors' hall is Valhalla."},
  {"cat":"literature","q":"Which Roman poet wrote the Aeneid?","o":["Ovid","Virgil","Horace","Homer"],"a":1,"e":"Virgil asked for the unfinished poem to be burned when he died, but Emperor Augustus refused."},
  {"cat": "literature", "q": "Which Jane Austen novel follows the Dashwood sisters, sensible Elinor and passionate Marianne?", "o": ["Emma", "Persuasion", "Sense and Sensibility", "Mansfield Park"], "a": 2, "e": "Published in 1811, it was her first novel, credited only to 'A Lady'."},
  {"cat":"literature","q":"Who wrote Charlotte's Web?","o":["Dr. Seuss","Roald Dahl","E. B. White","Beverly Cleary"],"a":2,"e":"The spider Charlotte spins words into her web, such as 'SOME PIG', to save Wilbur.","k":1}
);
WB.CLAIMS.push(
  {"c":"You need to drink eight glasses of water a day on top of everything else.","t":false,"e":"There's no solid science behind the eight-glass rule. Food, tea, coffee and other drinks all count, and for most healthy people thirst is a reliable guide."},
  {"c":"If you stop exercising, your muscle turns into fat.","t":false,"e":"Muscle and fat are different tissues, so one can't become the other. Without exercise muscles shrink, and if you eat the same amount, fat can build up."},
  {"c":"You can sweat out toxins.","t":false,"e":"Sweat is mostly water with a little salt, and its job is cooling you down. Your liver and kidneys do the real work of clearing waste."},
  {"c":"People having a seizure can swallow their tongue.","t":false,"e":"It's physically impossible to swallow your tongue. Never put anything in the person's mouth: time the seizure, cushion their head and call 000 if needed."},
  {"c":"Weeing on a jellyfish sting eases the pain.","t":false,"e":"Urine doesn't help and may make a sting worse. In Australia, vinegar is used for tropical box jellyfish stings, while bluebottle stings are best soaked in hot water."},
  {"c":"Earwax should be cleaned out with cotton buds.","t":false,"e":"Ears clean themselves: wax slowly moves outwards on its own. Cotton buds tend to push wax deeper and can damage the eardrum."},
  {"c":"You can't get sunburnt on a cloudy day.","t":false,"e":"Much of the Sun's UV gets through cloud, so you can still burn. In Australia, check the daily UV index rather than the sky.","k":1},
  {"c":"A suntan is a healthy way to protect your skin from burning.","t":false,"e":"A tan is a sign your skin cells have already been damaged by UV. It gives only very weak protection, far less than sunscreen."},
  {"c":"SPF 50 sunscreen blocks twice as much UV as SPF 25.","t":false,"e":"SPF 30 blocks about 97% of UVB and SPF 50 about 98%. The bigger difference comes from applying enough and reapplying often."},
  {"c":"Antibacterial soap is needed to properly clean your hands.","t":false,"e":"Studies have found plain soap and water works just as well for everyday handwashing. Soap lifts germs off your skin so they rinse away."},
  {"c":"Taking extra vitamins is always harmless, because your body gets rid of what it doesn't need.","t":false,"e":"Some vitamins, like A and D, are stored in the body and can build up to harmful levels. More is not always better."},
  {"c":"Sugar itself eats holes in your teeth.","t":false,"e":"Bacteria in your mouth feed on sugar and produce acid, and it's the acid that wears away tooth enamel. That's why brushing and less frequent snacking both help.","k":1},
  {"c":"Your heart stops when you sneeze.","t":false,"e":"A sneeze can briefly change pressure in your chest and alter your heartbeat rhythm, but your heart keeps beating.","k":1},
  {"c":"You're slightly taller in the morning than at night.","t":true,"e":"During the day, gravity squashes the soft discs between the bones of your spine. By evening you can be about 1 cm shorter, and you get it back overnight while lying down.","k":1},
  {"c":"Your small intestine is much longer than your large intestine.","t":true,"e":"The small intestine is about 6 metres long and the large intestine about 1.5 metres. The names refer to how wide they are, not how long."},
  {"c":"Your inner ears help you keep your balance.","t":true,"e":"Fluid-filled canals in your inner ear sense how your head moves. That's why an ear infection can make you feel dizzy."},
  {"c":"Your left lung is smaller than your right lung.","t":true,"e":"The left lung is smaller to make room for your heart. It has two sections, called lobes, while the right lung has three.","k":1},
  {"c":"Fingernails grow faster than toenails.","t":true,"e":"Fingernails grow roughly two to three times faster than toenails. A lost fingernail takes about six months to grow back fully.","k":1},
  {"c":"Fresh sweat has almost no smell.","t":true,"e":"Body odour comes from bacteria on the skin breaking down sweat, mostly from glands in the armpits. That's why washing makes such a difference."},
  {"c":"Most people have a dominant eye.","t":true,"e":"Just as you favour one hand, your brain relies slightly more on one eye. Frame a distant object between your hands and close each eye in turn to find yours."},
  {"c":"Natural and herbal remedies are always safe because they come from plants.","t":false,"e":"Many strong medicines and poisons come from plants. Herbal products can also interact with prescription medicines, so tell your doctor or pharmacist what you take."},
  {"c":"Cutting an earthworm in half creates two worms.","t":false,"e":"In some species the head end can survive and regrow a tail, but the tail end usually dies. You never get two whole worms.","k":1},
  {"c":"One dog year equals seven human years.","t":false,"e":"Dogs age very fast in their first year or two, then more slowly. Size matters too: large breeds tend to age faster than small ones.","k":1},
  {"c":"Koalas are a type of bear.","t":false,"e":"Koalas are marsupials, carrying their young in a pouch. The 'koala bear' name came from early European settlers.","k":1},
  {"c":"All bees die after they sting.","t":false,"e":"Honeybee workers usually die after stinging a mammal because their barbed sting gets stuck. Most other bees, and wasps, can sting again and again.","k":1},
  {"c":"Only female mosquitoes bite.","t":true,"e":"Females need protein from blood to develop their eggs. Males feed on nectar and plant juices instead.","k":1},
  {"c":"Wombats do cube-shaped poo.","t":true,"e":"Parts of a wombat's intestine walls stretch differently, which shapes its droppings into cubes. Wombats leave them on rocks and logs to mark their territory.","k":1},
  {"c":"Cheetahs can't roar.","t":true,"e":"Cheetahs chirp, purr and hiss, but they lack the voice box structure that lets lions and tigers roar.","k":1},
  {"c":"Koalas have fingerprints very like ours.","t":true,"e":"Koala fingerprints have loops and whorls so similar to human ones that they are hard to tell apart, even under a microscope.","k":1},
  {"c":"Penguins only live in icy places.","t":false,"e":"The Galápagos penguin lives near the equator, and little penguins live along southern Australia's coast. Phillip Island in Victoria is famous for them.","k":1},
  {"c":"Polar bears have black skin under their fur.","t":true,"e":"Their fur hairs are actually clear and hollow, and they look white because they scatter light. The black skin underneath helps soak up warmth."},
  {"c":"Rats laugh when they're tickled.","t":true,"e":"Researchers found rats make high-pitched chirps when tickled, too high for humans to hear. Tickled rats even chase the researcher's hand for more.","k":1},
  {"c":"Flamingos are born pink.","t":false,"e":"Flamingo chicks hatch grey or white. Their pink colour comes from pigments in the algae and shrimp they eat.","k":1},
  {"c":"Snails have teeth.","t":true,"e":"A snail's tongue-like ribbon, called a radula, is covered in thousands of microscopic teeth. It uses them to scrape up food.","k":1},
  {"c":"Bulls are made angry by the colour red.","t":false,"e":"Bulls can't tell red from green. It's the movement of the matador's cape that makes them charge, not its colour.","k":1},
  {"c":"Sharks have skeletons made of bone.","t":false,"e":"A shark's skeleton is made of cartilage, the same bendy material in your ears and nose. It's lighter than bone, which helps sharks stay buoyant.","k":1},
  {"c":"Cats can't taste sweetness.","t":true,"e":"Cats lack a working version of the gene for the sweet taste receptor. As strict meat-eaters, they don't need it."},
  {"c":"Chocolate is poisonous to dogs.","t":true,"e":"Chocolate contains theobromine, which dogs process very slowly. Dark chocolate and cooking chocolate are the most dangerous.","k":1},
  {"c":"Redback spider bites often kill people in Australia.","t":false,"e":"No one in Australia is known to have died from a redback bite since antivenom arrived in the 1950s. Bites can still be very painful, so seek medical help."},
  {"c":"Australia's wild rabbit plague began with about two dozen rabbits released for hunting.","t":true,"e":"In 1859, Thomas Austin released 24 rabbits on his property near Geelong. Within decades there were hundreds of millions across the country."},
  {"c":"Most of Australia's native mammals are found nowhere else on Earth.","t":true,"e":"More than 80% of Australia's mammals are endemic, including kangaroos, koalas, wombats and platypuses. Long isolation let them evolve separately."},
  {"c": "A wombat can sprint at up to about 40 km/h.", "t": true, "e": "Despite their stocky build and short legs, wombats can briefly outrun most people over a short distance."},
  {"c":"Giant pandas have the digestive system of a meat-eater.","t":true,"e":"Pandas are classed as carnivores, but they eat mostly bamboo. Their gut digests it poorly, so they spend up to around 14 hours a day eating."},
  {"c":"Dingoes arrived in Australia long after the first people.","t":true,"e":"The oldest known dingo remains are a little over 3,000 years old, while people have lived in Australia for tens of thousands of years. Dingoes were probably brought by seafaring visitors."},
  {"c":"Adult elephants can't jump.","t":true,"e":"Elephants are very heavy and their leg bones point straight down, so they can't push off the ground with all four feet at once. They can still walk surprisingly fast.","k":1},
  {"c":"Some ants farm other insects.","t":true,"e":"Many ants protect aphids and 'milk' them for the sweet honeydew they produce. The ants guard them from predators in return."},
  {"c":"Killer whales are actually a type of dolphin.","t":true,"e":"Orcas are the largest member of the ocean dolphin family. The name 'killer whale' likely came from sailors who saw them hunting whales.","k":1},
  {"c":"Astronauts float on the space station because there's no gravity there.","t":false,"e":"At the station's height, gravity is still about 90% as strong as on the ground. Astronauts float because they and the station are always falling around Earth together.","k":1},
  {"c":"Footprints left on the Moon could last for millions of years.","t":true,"e":"The Moon has no wind or rain to wear them away, so the Apollo astronauts' footprints could last millions of years unless small space rocks disturb them.","k":1},
  {"c":"The Moon looks bigger near the horizon because it's closer to us then.","t":false,"e":"It's an illusion: the Moon is actually slightly further away when rising. Measure it with a coin at arm's length and it's the same size."},
  {"c":"Earth is a perfect sphere.","t":false,"e":"Earth bulges slightly at the equator because it spins. It's about 43 km wider across the equator than from pole to pole.","k":1},
  {"c":"Black holes suck in everything around them like cosmic vacuum cleaners.","t":false,"e":"From a distance, a black hole pulls just like any object of the same mass. If the Sun were swapped for a black hole of equal mass, Earth would keep the same orbit."},
  {"c":"Venus spins in the opposite direction to most planets.","t":true,"e":"On Venus, the Sun rises in the west and sets in the east. Uranus also has an unusual spin, tipped over on its side."},
  {"c":"You can see the International Space Station without a telescope.","t":true,"e":"It reflects sunlight and looks like a bright, steady star gliding across the sky in a few minutes, usually just after sunset or before sunrise."},
  {"c":"Heavier objects fall faster than lighter ones.","t":false,"e":"Without air resistance, everything falls at the same rate. An Apollo 15 astronaut dropped a hammer and a feather on the Moon and they landed together.","k":1},
  {"c":"Pure water is a good conductor of electricity.","t":false,"e":"Pure water conducts electricity poorly. It's the salts and minerals dissolved in everyday water that carry the current, which is why water and electricity are still a deadly mix."},
  {"c":"Raindrops are shaped like teardrops.","t":false,"e":"Small raindrops are round, and larger ones flatten on the bottom as they fall, a bit like a hamburger bun.","k":1},
  {"c":"Greenland is bigger than Australia.","t":false,"e":"Australia is more than three times bigger. Common world maps stretch areas near the poles, which makes Greenland look huge.","k":1},
  {"c":"Rubber tyres protect you from lightning when you're in a car.","t":false,"e":"A few centimetres of rubber can't stop lightning. A hard-topped car protects you because its metal body carries the current around you to the ground."},
  {"c":"Your body contains enough iron to make a small nail.","t":true,"e":"An adult has about 3 to 4 grams of iron, mostly in the haemoglobin that carries oxygen in your blood."},
  {"c":"Most of Earth's fresh water is frozen.","t":true,"e":"Around two-thirds of the world's fresh water is locked up in ice caps and glaciers, mostly in Antarctica and Greenland."},
  {"c":"Australia is the driest inhabited continent.","t":true,"e":"Only Antarctica is drier. Much of inland Australia gets less than 250 mm of rain a year."},
  {"c":"Cooking always burns off all the alcohol in a dish.","t":false,"e":"Plenty can remain, depending on the method and time. Studies found a dish simmered for 30 minutes can still hold about a third of its alcohol."},
  {"c":"Most 'wasabi' served outside Japan is actually horseradish.","t":true,"e":"Real wasabi is expensive and hard to grow. Most of the green paste is horseradish mixed with mustard and green colouring."},
  {"c":"Each cashew nut grows on the outside of a fruit.","t":true,"e":"A cashew hangs from the bottom of a fleshy 'cashew apple'. The nut sits inside a shell containing an irritating oil, so it has to be processed carefully."},
  {"c":"Bananas grow on trees.","t":false,"e":"Banana plants look like trees, but they have no wooden trunk. Botanically, they're giant herbs.","k":1},
  {"c":"Almonds are closely related to peaches.","t":true,"e":"Both belong to the same plant group, Prunus, along with plums and cherries. The almond we eat is the seed inside the fruit's stone."},
  {"c":"A very fresh egg will sink in a glass of water.","t":true,"e":"As an egg ages, moisture escapes through the shell and the air pocket inside grows. Older eggs tilt upwards or float.","k":1},
  {"c":"You should wash raw chicken before cooking it.","t":false,"e":"Washing chicken can splash bacteria around your sink and kitchen. Proper cooking kills the germs anyway."},
  {"c":"Worcestershire sauce is made with fish.","t":true,"e":"Traditional Worcestershire sauce contains fermented anchovies, along with vinegar, molasses, tamarind and spices."},
  {"c":"Brown eggs are more nutritious than white eggs.","t":false,"e":"Shell colour depends on the breed of hen. Inside, brown and white eggs are nutritionally the same."},
  {"c":"Apples float in water because they contain lots of air.","t":true,"e":"About a quarter of an apple's volume is air trapped between its cells. That makes them less dense than water, which is why apple bobbing works.","k":1},
  {"c":"Vanilla comes from an orchid.","t":true,"e":"Vanilla beans are the seed pods of a climbing orchid. Each flower must usually be hand-pollinated, which helps explain vanilla's high price."},
  {"c":"Moving into a higher tax bracket means all your income is taxed at the higher rate.","t":false,"e":"Australia uses marginal rates: only the income above each threshold is taxed at that bracket's rate. Tipping into a higher bracket only affects the extra dollars."},
  {"c":"Checking your own credit score lowers it.","t":false,"e":"Checking your own score is a 'soft' enquiry that doesn't affect it. Applications for new credit are what show up on your file."},
  {"c":"If an investment falls 50%, it needs to rise 50% to get back to where it started.","t":false,"e":"It needs to double, a 100% gain. $100 that drops to $50 must gain another $50, which is 100% of $50."},
  {"c":"Australian shops must accept any number of coins as payment.","t":false,"e":"Coins are only legal tender up to set limits. For example, 5c to 50c coins can be refused for amounts over $5."},
  {"c":"Australia's 1c and 2c coins were taken out of circulation in the early 1990s.","t":true,"e":"They were withdrawn in 1992. Cash totals are now rounded to the nearest 5 cents, while card payments are still charged to the exact cent."},
  {"c":"Australia's first coins were made by punching holes in Spanish dollars.","t":true,"e":"In 1813, Governor Macquarie had the centres punched out of Spanish silver dollars. The ring became the 'holey dollar' and the centre the 'dump', giving the colony two coins."},
  {"c":"The Australian $2 coin is smaller than the $1 coin.","t":true,"e":"The $2 coin replaced the $2 note in 1988. It is worth more than the $1 coin but is noticeably smaller.","k":1},
  {"c":"The numbers 1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6 are less likely to win Lotto than a random mix.","t":false,"e":"Every combination has exactly the same chance. The only catch is that many people pick those numbers, so you'd share the prize."},
  {"c":"Australian lenders advertising a home loan interest rate must also show a 'comparison rate'.","t":true,"e":"The comparison rate combines the interest rate with most fees and charges into one figure. It makes the true cost of different loans easier to compare."},
  {"c":"The word 'mortgage' literally means 'death pledge'.","t":true,"e":"It comes from Old French. The pledge 'dies' when the debt is paid off, or when the borrower fails to pay and loses the property."},
  {"c":"Listening to Mozart makes babies smarter.","t":false,"e":"The 'Mozart effect' came from a small 1993 study of adults, showing only a brief boost on one type of task. Later research didn't support lasting effects."},
  {"c":"Lie detector tests reliably catch liars.","t":false,"e":"Polygraphs measure signs of stress, like heart rate and sweating, not lies. Honest people can fail from nerves, which is why courts rarely accept them."},
  {"c":"A famous 1957 experiment proved hidden 'Eat popcorn' messages in films boosted sales.","t":false,"e":"The marketer behind it, James Vicary, later admitted the results were made up. Attempts to repeat it failed."},
  {"c":"Your memory records events like a video camera.","t":false,"e":"Each time you recall something, your brain rebuilds it. Details can shift or be added without you noticing."},
  {"c":"Humans now have a shorter attention span than a goldfish.","t":false,"e":"That widely shared claim has no scientific source behind it. Attention depends heavily on the task and how interested you are."},
  {"c":"Liars usually avoid eye contact.","t":false,"e":"Large reviews of deception research show gaze aversion isn't a reliable sign of lying. Many liars hold eye contact deliberately."},
  {"c":"Hospitals get busier during a full moon.","t":false,"e":"Studies of emergency departments and hospital admissions have found no link to the full moon. We just tend to notice busy nights that fit the story."},
  {"c":"Your handwriting reveals your personality.","t":false,"e":"Graphology, the practice of reading personality from handwriting, has failed scientific tests. It performs no better than chance."},
  {"c":"Noticing something everywhere right after you learn about it has a name.","t":true,"e":"It's called the frequency illusion, or Baader-Meinhof phenomenon. The thing isn't more common: your attention is now tuned to spot it."},
  {"c":"'Goodbye' comes from 'God be with ye'.","t":true,"e":"Over centuries the phrase was shortened to 'godbwye'. 'Good' later replaced 'God', perhaps influenced by 'good day'."},
  {"c":"'Posh' comes from 'Port Out, Starboard Home', about the best cabins on ships to India.","t":false,"e":"There's no evidence for this story, and no tickets stamped 'POSH' have been found. The word's real origin is unknown."},
  {"c":"The word 'quiz' was invented as a bet by a Dublin theatre owner.","t":false,"e":"The tale of a man paying children to scrawl 'quiz' around Dublin only appeared decades later. The word was already in use before the supposed bet."},
  {"c":"'Sincere' comes from the Latin 'sine cera', meaning 'without wax'.","t":false,"e":"This popular story about sculptors hiding cracks with wax is a folk tale. The word comes from the Latin 'sincerus', meaning pure or clean."},
  {"c":"The word 'nice' once meant 'foolish'.","t":true,"e":"It comes from the Latin 'nescius', meaning ignorant. Over centuries it shifted through meanings like 'fussy' and 'precise' before becoming 'pleasant'."},
  {"c":"The word 'muscle' comes from the Latin for 'little mouse'.","t":true,"e":"Romans thought a flexing muscle looked like a mouse moving under the skin, so they called it 'musculus'."},
  {"c":"The word 'alphabet' comes from the first two Greek letters.","t":true,"e":"Alpha and beta are the first two letters of the Greek alphabet. In English we have a similar name: learning your 'ABCs'.","k":1},
  {"c":"The word 'checkmate' comes from Persian.","t":true,"e":"It comes from the Persian 'shāh māt', usually translated as 'the king is helpless' or 'the king is defeated'. Chess reached Europe through Persia and the Arab world."},
  {"c":"Sign language is the same all over the world.","t":false,"e":"There are hundreds of sign languages. Australia's is Auslan, which is closer to British Sign Language than to American Sign Language."},
  {"c":"'Emoji' comes from the English word 'emotion'.","t":false,"e":"It's Japanese: 'e' means picture and 'moji' means character. The resemblance to 'emotion' is a coincidence."},
  {"c":"The word 'clue' comes from a ball of thread.","t":true,"e":"A 'clew' was a ball of yarn. In Greek myth, Theseus used a thread to find his way out of the Labyrinth, so a clew came to mean something that guides you to an answer."},
  {"c":"The word 'disaster' literally means 'bad star'.","t":true,"e":"It comes from the Italian 'disastro', from 'dis' (bad) and 'astro' (star). Bad events were once blamed on unlucky positions of the stars and planets."},
  {"c":"The English word 'girl' once meant a child of either sex.","t":true,"e":"In Middle English, 'girl' could mean any young person. Boys were sometimes called 'knave girls'."},
  {"c":"The abbreviation 'lb' for pound comes from Latin.","t":true,"e":"It stands for 'libra', an ancient Roman unit of weight. In Latin 'libra' also meant scales, which is where the star sign Libra gets its name."},
  {"c":"Uluru has two official names.","t":true,"e":"Since 1993 it has had a dual name, and since 2002 it has been officially 'Uluru / Ayers Rock'. Uluru is its Anangu name."},
  {"c":"The first programmers of the ENIAC computer were women.","t":true,"e":"Six women programmed ENIAC, one of the first general-purpose electronic computers, in the 1940s. They worked it out largely from wiring diagrams."},
  {"c":"The first computer mouse was made of wood.","t":true,"e":"Douglas Engelbart's team built it in the 1960s from a wooden block with two metal wheels underneath.","k":1},
  {"c":"Bluetooth is named after a Viking-era king.","t":true,"e":"Harald 'Bluetooth' Gormsson united parts of Denmark and Norway. The technology was meant to unite devices, and its logo combines his initials in runes.","k":1},
  {"c":"The first text message ever sent said 'Merry Christmas'.","t":true,"e":"Engineer Neil Papworth sent it from a computer to a mobile phone in the UK in December 1992."},
  {"c":"Deleting a file immediately wipes it from your computer's drive.","t":false,"e":"Usually the computer just marks the space as free to reuse. Until it's written over, the data can often be recovered, so use proper wiping tools for sensitive files."},
  {"c":"The internet and the World Wide Web are the same thing.","t":false,"e":"The internet is the global network connecting computers. The Web, invented by Tim Berners-Lee around 1989, is one service that runs on it, alongside things like email."},
  {"c":"reCAPTCHA puzzles once helped digitise old books.","t":true,"e":"When you typed hard-to-read words, you were often helping computers read scanned books and newspaper archives that software couldn't decipher."},
  {"c":"Email is older than the World Wide Web.","t":true,"e":"Ray Tomlinson sent the first network email between computers in 1971, about two decades before the Web appeared. It ran on ARPANET, a US research network that grew into the internet."},
  {"c":"Junk email is called 'spam' because of a Monty Python sketch.","t":true,"e":"In the 1970 sketch, Vikings in a café drown out conversation by singing about Spam. Early internet users borrowed it for floods of unwanted messages."},
  {"c":"People convicted in the Salem witch trials were burnt at the stake.","t":false,"e":"In Salem in 1692, those executed were hanged, and one man was pressed to death with stones. Burning was used for witchcraft in parts of Europe, not in colonial Massachusetts."},
  {"c":"In the Middle Ages, most people died by about 30.","t":false,"e":"Average lifespans were low mainly because so many children died young. People who survived childhood often lived into their 50s, 60s or beyond."},
  {"c":"Iron maidens were a common medieval torture device.","t":false,"e":"There's no good evidence they were used in the Middle Ages. Most surviving examples were put together in the 1700s and 1800s for display."},
  {"c":"An apple falling on Isaac Newton's head inspired his theory of gravity.","t":false,"e":"Newton said watching an apple fall in a garden got him thinking about gravity. There's no evidence it hit him on the head."},
  {"c":"George Washington had wooden false teeth.","t":false,"e":"His dentures were made from materials like ivory, metal, and human and animal teeth. Stains in the ivory may have made them look like wood grain."},
  {"c":"Roman gladiators nearly always fought to the death.","t":false,"e":"Gladiators were expensive to train and feed, so many bouts ended without a death. A defeated fighter could be spared."},
  {"c":"Medieval knights in armour had to be lifted onto their horses with cranes.","t":false,"e":"Plate armour weighed about 15 to 25 kg, spread across the body. Knights could mount horses, run and even do acrobatics in it.","k":1},
  {"c":"Viking warriors wore horned helmets into battle.","t":false,"e":"No horned helmet has ever been found from a Viking-age warrior. The image became popular in the 1800s, helped by costumes for Wagner's operas."},
  {"c":"Julius Caesar was born by caesarean section.","t":false,"e":"His mother Aurelia lived for decades after his birth, which would have been very unlikely after the surgery in Roman times. The name's origin is uncertain."},
  {"c":"Marco Polo brought pasta to Italy from China.","t":false,"e":"Pasta was already known in Italy before Polo returned in 1295. The story was spread by a 1920s American pasta industry magazine."},
  {"c":"Harvard University is older than calculus.","t":true,"e":"Harvard was founded in 1636. Newton and Leibniz developed calculus independently several decades later."},
  {"c":"The fax machine was invented before the telephone.","t":true,"e":"Scottish inventor Alexander Bain patented an early fax design in 1843, more than 30 years before Bell's 1876 telephone patent."},
  {"c":"Albert Einstein was offered the presidency of Israel.","t":true,"e":"In 1952 he was invited to become Israel's president, a largely ceremonial role. He politely declined, saying he lacked the right skills."},
  {"c":"The First Fleet set up its settlement at Botany Bay.","t":false,"e":"The fleet arrived at Botany Bay in January 1788, but found it unsuitable. They moved north to Port Jackson and landed at Sydney Cove on 26 January."},
  {"c":"All boomerangs are designed to come back.","t":false,"e":"Most boomerangs used for hunting and fighting were non-returning and flew long and straight. Returning boomerangs were mostly used for sport and play.","k":1},
  {"c":"Galileo invented the telescope.","t":false,"e":"The first known telescope patent was sought by a Dutch spectacle maker in 1608. Galileo built improved versions and was among the first to study the sky with one."},
  {"c":"Christopher Columbus never set foot on the mainland of what is now the United States.","t":true,"e":"On his four voyages he reached Caribbean islands, including Puerto Rico, now a US territory, and the coasts of Central and South America, but never the North American mainland."},
  {"c":"Napoleon's soldiers shot the nose off the Great Sphinx.","t":false,"e":"Drawings from decades before Napoleon's 1798 arrival already show the Sphinx without its nose. Marks suggest it was deliberately chiselled off centuries earlier."},
  {"c":"The Olympic torch relay dates back to ancient Greece.","t":false,"e":"The ancient Games had sacred flames, but the torch relay to the host city was introduced for the 1936 Berlin Olympics."},
  {"c":"A man on horseback cut the ribbon before the official opening of the Sydney Harbour Bridge.","t":true,"e":"In 1932, Francis de Groot rode forward and slashed the ribbon with his sword before the Premier could cut it. The ribbon was retied and cut again."},
  {"c":"The largest alluvial gold nugget ever found was dug up in Victoria.","t":true,"e":"The 'Welcome Stranger' was found near Moliagul in 1869, just below the surface. It held about 70 kg of gold."},
  {"c":"Most convicts sent to Australia were serious violent criminals.","t":false,"e":"Most were transported for theft and other property crimes, often fairly minor. Many sentences were seven years."},
  {"c":"The Sydney Opera House roof is covered in more than a million tiles.","t":true,"e":"About 1,056,000 glazed tiles cover the sails. They were made in Sweden and designed to be self-cleaning in the rain.","k":1},
  {"c":"Australia has more than 10,000 beaches.","t":true,"e":"Surveys have counted over 10,000 beaches along the mainland and Tasmanian coasts. You could visit a new one every day for over 27 years.","k":1}
);
WB.PUZZLES.push(
  {"q":"Anna can paint a fence in 3 hours. Ben can paint the same fence in 6 hours. Working together, how long will it take them?","o":["4.5 hours","2 hours","3 hours","1.5 hours"],"a":1,"e":"Anna paints 1/3 of the fence per hour and Ben 1/6, so together they do 1/3 + 1/6 = 1/2 per hour. Half a fence an hour means 2 hours. Averaging the two times (4.5 hours) is the classic trap."},
  {"q":"A tap fills an empty tank in 4 hours. A drain empties a full tank in 6 hours. If both are open, how long does the empty tank take to fill?","o":["12 hours","2 hours","10 hours","24 hours"],"a":0,"e":"Each hour the tap adds 1/4 of the tank and the drain removes 1/6, a net gain of 3/12 - 2/12 = 1/12. So it takes 12 hours to fill."},
  {"q":"A mother is three times as old as her daughter. In 12 years, she will be exactly twice as old. How old is the daughter now?","o":["10","14","16","12"],"a":3,"e":"If the daughter is d, then 3d + 12 = 2(d + 12), so d = 12. Check: 12 and 36 now, then 24 and 48 in 12 years."},
  {"q":"A car travels at a steady 90 km/h. How far does it go in 20 minutes?","o":["18 km","45 km","30 km","20 km"],"a":2,"e":"20 minutes is one third of an hour, and one third of 90 km is 30 km.","k":1},
  {"q":"You roll one ordinary six-sided die. What is the chance of rolling a number greater than 4?","o":["1 in 2","1 in 3","1 in 6","2 in 3"],"a":1,"e":"Only 5 and 6 are greater than 4. That's 2 of the 6 faces, which is 1 in 3.","k":1},
  {"q":"A rooster lays an egg right on the peak of a sloping roof. Which side does the egg roll down?","o":["Neither: roosters don't lay eggs","The steeper side","The side facing the wind","It stays balanced on top"],"a":0,"e":"Roosters are male chickens, so there is no egg. Only hens lay eggs.","k":1},
  {"q":"What comes next: 2, 3, 5, 7, 11, 13, ?","o":["15","19","16","17"],"a":3,"e":"These are the prime numbers, which divide evenly only by 1 and themselves. 15 and 16 aren't prime, and 17 comes before 19."},
  {"q":"What comes next: 1, 2, 4, 7, 11, 16, ?","o":["21","23","22","26"],"a":2,"e":"The gaps grow by one each time: +1, +2, +3, +4, +5, so the next step is +6, and 16 + 6 = 22."},
  {"q":"What comes next: 1, 8, 27, 64, ?","o":["100","125","81","128"],"a":1,"e":"These are cubes: 1x1x1, 2x2x2, 3x3x3, 4x4x4. Next is 5 x 5 x 5 = 125."},
  {"q":"What comes next: 3, 6, 11, 18, 27, ?","o":["38","36","37","40"],"a":0,"e":"The gaps are 3, 5, 7, 9, so the next is 11, giving 38. Each term is also a square number plus 2 (36 + 2 = 38)."},
  {"q":"What comes next: Z, X, V, T, R, ?","o":["Q","O","S","P"],"a":3,"e":"The pattern goes backwards through the alphabet, skipping one letter each time. After R, skip Q and land on P.","k":1},
  {"q":"What comes next: 1, 2, 6, 24, 120, ?","o":["240","600","720","144"],"a":2,"e":"Each number is multiplied by the next whole number: x2, x3, x4, x5. So 120 x 6 = 720. These are called factorials."},
  {"q":"All bloops are razzies. All razzies are lazzies. Are all bloops definitely lazzies?","o":["No, never","Yes, definitely","Only some of them","There's no way to tell"],"a":1,"e":"If every bloop is inside the razzie group, and every razzie is inside the lazzie group, then every bloop must be a lazzie too. The made-up words stop you relying on what you already know."},
  {"q":"'If it rains, the grass gets wet.' You look out and the grass is wet. What can you be sure of?","o":["Not that it rained: something else could have wet it","It definitely rained","It definitely didn't rain","It will rain soon"],"a":0,"e":"A sprinkler or morning dew could also wet the grass. Reasoning backwards like this is a common error called 'affirming the consequent'."},
  {"q":"On an island, knights always tell the truth and knaves always lie. A says: 'B is a knave.' B says: 'A and I are the same type.' What are they?","o":["Both are knights","Both are knaves","A is a knave, B is a knight","A is a knight, B is a knave"],"a":3,"e":"If A were a knave, B would be a knight, but then B's claim that they match would be false, which a knight can't say. So A is a knight, B is a knave, and B's claim is a lie, as it should be."},
  {"q":"Someone ate the last biscuit. Amy says: 'Ben ate it.' Ben says: 'Cal ate it.' Cal says: 'Ben is lying.' Exactly one of them is lying. Who ate it?","o":["Amy","Cal","Ben","It's impossible to tell"],"a":2,"e":"If Ben ate it, Amy tells the truth, Ben lies and Cal (who says Ben is lying) tells the truth: exactly one liar. Testing Amy or Cal as the eater gives two liars each time.","k":1},
  {"q":"Four friends stand in a queue. Kim is ahead of Nia. Nia is ahead of Lee. Max is behind Lee. Who is second in line?","o":["Kim","Nia","Lee","Max"],"a":1,"e":"Put the clues together and the only possible order is Kim, Nia, Lee, Max. So Nia is second.","k":1},
  {"q":"You have a balance scale and just three weights: 1 kg, 3 kg and 9 kg. Weights can go on either pan. What is the heaviest whole-kilogram load you can weigh exactly?","o":["13 kg","9 kg","12 kg","15 kg"],"a":0,"e":"All three together make 1 + 3 + 9 = 13 kg. Putting weights on the load's pan fills every gap below that too, for example 2 kg = 3 kg on one side against 1 kg plus the load on the other."},
  {"q":"There are 10 bags of coins. Real coins weigh 10 g, but one whole bag holds fakes weighing 11 g each. With a digital scale, what is the fewest weighings needed to find the fake bag?","o":["2","4","10","1"],"a":3,"e":"Take 1 coin from bag 1, 2 from bag 2, and so on up to 10 from bag 10, and weigh them all at once. Every extra gram over 550 g points to a bag: 3 extra grams means bag 3."},
  {"q":"An adult and two children must cross a river. The boat carries either one adult alone, or up to two children. What is the fewest one-way crossings needed to get everyone across?","o":["3","4","5","7"],"a":2,"e":"Both children cross (1), one rows back (2), the adult crosses (3), the other child rows back (4), and both children cross again (5). The boat always needs a child on the far side to bring it back."},
  {"q":"What is the smallest number of people you need in a room to be certain that at least two share a birth month?","o":["12","13","24","7"],"a":1,"e":"With 12 people, everyone could be born in a different month. The 13th person must match someone. This is the 'pigeonhole principle'."},
  {"q":"You draw cards from a shuffled 52-card deck without looking. How many must you draw to be certain of getting two of the same suit?","o":["5","2","4","13"],"a":0,"e":"There are only 4 suits, so 4 cards could all be different. The 5th card must match one of them."},
  {"q":"A clock loses 10 minutes every hour. It is set correctly at noon. What does it show when the real time is 6:00 pm?","o":["5:50 pm","5:10 pm","6:10 pm","5:00 pm"],"a":3,"e":"Six hours pass, and the clock loses 10 minutes in each, 60 minutes in total. So it is a full hour behind."},
  {"q":"You glance at an ordinary clock in a mirror. The reflection seems to show 3:00. What is the real time?","o":["3:00","6:00","9:00","12:15"],"a":2,"e":"A mirror flips left and right. The hour hand that appears to point right (at 3) really points left (at 9), while the minute hand at 12 looks the same.","k":1},
  {"q":"Today is Wednesday. What day of the week will it be in 100 days?","o":["Thursday","Friday","Saturday","Monday"],"a":1,"e":"100 days is 14 full weeks (98 days) plus 2 days. Full weeks change nothing, so it's two days after Wednesday."},
  {"q":"Today is Monday. What day was it three days before the day after tomorrow?","o":["Sunday","Saturday","Monday","Friday"],"a":0,"e":"The day after tomorrow is Wednesday. Three days before Wednesday is Sunday.","k":1},
  {"q":"You buy an old book for $12 and sell it for $15. Then you buy it back for $18 and sell it again for $21. Overall, how much money did you make?","o":["$3","$0","$9","$6"],"a":3,"e":"Treat it as two separate deals: $3 profit on the first and $3 profit on the second, $6 in total. Adding up all money in and out gives the same: 15 + 21 - 12 - 18 = 6."},
  {"q":"You can walk to work at 5 km/h in 36 minutes. How long would the same trip take by bike at 15 km/h?","o":["18 minutes","24 minutes","12 minutes","10 minutes"],"a":2,"e":"The bike is three times as fast, so it takes a third of the time. (36 minutes at 5 km/h is a 3 km trip, and 3 km at 15 km/h is 12 minutes.)","k":1},
  {"q":"A 200-metre train travels at 72 km/h through a 400-metre tunnel. How long from the moment its front enters until its back leaves?","o":["20 seconds","30 seconds","10 seconds","60 seconds"],"a":1,"e":"72 km/h is 20 metres per second. The front must travel the tunnel's 400 m plus the train's own 200 m, so 600 m at 20 m/s takes 30 seconds."},
  {"q":"A hen and a half lays an egg and a half in a day and a half. At that rate, how many eggs does one hen lay in 6 days?","o":["4","6","3","9"],"a":0,"e":"Two-thirds of the hens lay two-thirds of the eggs in the same time, so one hen lays one egg in a day and a half. Six days holds four lots of a day and a half, so four eggs."},
  {"q":"Your average after four tests is 80. What do you need on the fifth test to lift your average to 82?","o":["82","84","92","90"],"a":3,"e":"Four tests at 80 total 320. An average of 82 over five tests needs 410 points, so the fifth must be 410 - 320 = 90."},
  {"q":"Count carefully: how many letters are in the two words 'the alphabet'?","o":["26","8","11","10"],"a":2,"e":"Count the letters in the actual words: 'the' has 3 and 'alphabet' has 8, which makes 11.","k":1},
  {"q":"An electric train is travelling north. A strong wind is blowing from the west. Which way does the train's smoke blow?","o":["East","There isn't any smoke","North-east","South"],"a":1,"e":"Electric trains draw power from overhead wires or a live rail and don't burn fuel, so there's no smoke.","k":1},
  {"q":"What can you hold in your right hand, but never in your left hand?","o":["Your left elbow","Your right elbow","A pencil","Your nose"],"a":0,"e":"Your right hand can cup your left elbow, but your left hand can never reach its own elbow. Try it.","k":1},
  {"q":"Before Mount Everest was measured and recognised, what was the highest mountain on Earth?","o":["K2","Kangchenjunga","Mont Blanc","Mount Everest"],"a":3,"e":"The mountain was always the tallest; people just hadn't measured it yet. British surveyors identified it as the highest peak in the 1850s.","k":1},
  {"q":"A man lives on the 10th floor. Each morning he takes the lift all the way down. Coming home, he rides only to the 7th floor and walks the rest, unless it's raining. Why?","o":["He wants the exercise on dry days","The lift is broken above floor 7","He's too short to reach the higher buttons, except with his umbrella","He visits a friend on floor 7"],"a":2,"e":"Only the umbrella explains the rain rule: on wet days he can use it to press the '10' button. Lateral puzzles reward finding the one detail every answer must explain."},
  {"q":"I'm a two-digit number. My digits add up to 9, and my tens digit is twice my ones digit. What number am I?","o":["36","63","54","72"],"a":1,"e":"Pairs adding to 9 are 9-0, 8-1, 7-2, 6-3 and 5-4. Only in 63 is the first digit twice the second.","k":1},
  {"q":"Think of any number. Double it, add 10, halve the result, then subtract the number you first thought of. What do you get?","o":["5","10","It depends on your number","0"],"a":0,"e":"Doubling and halving cancel out, and so do adding and subtracting your number. All that survives is half of the 10 you added.","k":1},
  {"q":"If you write out every whole number from 1 to 100, how many times do you write the digit 7?","o":["10","11","19","20"],"a":3,"e":"There are ten 7s in the ones place (7, 17, ... 97) and ten in the tens place (70 to 79). 77 lands in both groups, which is right, because it contains two 7s."},
  {"q":"How many zeros are at the end of 1 x 2 x 3 x ... x 20 (every whole number from 1 to 20 multiplied together)?","o":["2","5","4","10"],"a":2,"e":"Each zero needs a 10, which is 2 x 5. There are plenty of 2s but only four 5s (from 5, 10, 15 and 20), so there are four zeros."},
  {"q":"What is the last digit of 2 multiplied by itself 100 times (2 to the power of 100)?","o":["2","6","4","8"],"a":1,"e":"Powers of 2 end in a repeating cycle: 2, 4, 8, 6, 2, 4, 8, 6 ... The cycle length is 4 and 100 divides exactly by 4, so it lands on the last one, 6."},
  {"q":"A bag holds 3 red marbles and 1 blue marble. You pull out two without looking. What is the chance both are red?","o":["1 in 2","1 in 4","3 in 4","9 in 16"],"a":0,"e":"The first is red 3/4 of the time, then the second is red 2/3 of the time, and 3/4 x 2/3 = 1/2. Another way: of the 6 possible pairs, 3 are both red."},
  {"q":"You flip a fair coin three times. What is the chance of getting at least one head?","o":["1 in 2","3 in 4","1 in 8","7 in 8"],"a":3,"e":"The only way to get no heads is tails three times, a 1/2 x 1/2 x 1/2 = 1/8 chance. Everything else has a head, so 7/8."},
  {"q":"In a lottery where 6 numbers are drawn, is the ticket 1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6 less likely to win than a random-looking ticket?","o":["Yes, far less likely","Yes, slightly less likely","No, every combination is equally likely","No, it's more likely"],"a":2,"e":"The balls don't know about patterns, so every set of six has exactly the same chance. Patterned tickets are still unwise for one reason: many people pick them, so a win would be shared."},
  {"q":"A game costs $2 to play. You roll a die and win as many dollars as the number shown. If you play many times, what happens on average?","o":["You break even","You gain $1.50 per game","You lose 50 cents per game","You gain $3.50 per game"],"a":1,"e":"The average roll is (1 + 2 + 3 + 4 + 5 + 6) / 6 = 3.5, so you win $3.50 on average for a $2 fee, a gain of $1.50 per game."},
  {"q":"A girl points at a boy and says: 'That boy's mother is my mother's only daughter.' Who is the boy?","o":["Her son","Her brother","Her cousin","Her nephew"],"a":0,"e":"Her mother's only daughter must be the girl herself. So she is the boy's mother."},
  {"q":"Tom's grandfather has 3 children, and each of them has exactly 2 children. How many cousins does Tom have on that side of the family?","o":["6","5","3","4"],"a":3,"e":"There are 6 grandchildren in total. Tom is one, and his only sibling is another, which leaves 4 cousins."},
  {"q":"What is the most pieces you can cut a flat round pizza into with 3 straight cuts, without moving any pieces?","o":["6","8","7","9"],"a":2,"e":"Cuts through the centre give only 6. Each new cut adds one piece, plus one more for every earlier cut it crosses inside the pizza: 2, then 4, then 7."},
  {"q":"Walking up the stairs from floor 1 to floor 3 takes 30 seconds. At the same pace, how long from floor 1 to floor 6?","o":["60 seconds","75 seconds","90 seconds","45 seconds"],"a":1,"e":"Floor 1 to 3 is only 2 flights, so each flight takes 15 seconds. Floor 1 to 6 is 5 flights: 75 seconds."},
  {"q":"What is the fewest straight cuts needed to divide a round cake into 8 equal pieces, if you can't move pieces between cuts?","o":["3","4","6","7"],"a":0,"e":"Two cuts from above in a cross make 4 quarters, then one horizontal cut through the middle of the cake's height turns them into 8. Four cuts from above would also work, but 3 is fewer."},
  {"q":"In 3 years, Emma will be twice as old as she was 3 years ago. How old is Emma now?","o":["6","12","8","9"],"a":3,"e":"If Emma is x, then x + 3 = 2(x - 3), so x = 9. Check: in 3 years she'll be 12, and 3 years ago she was 6.","k":1},
  {"q":"When I was 6, my brother was half my age. Now I'm 40. How old is my brother?","o":["20","34","37","43"],"a":2,"e":"When I was 6 he was 3, so he's 3 years younger. The gap never changes, so he's 37. Halving 40 is the trap."},
  {"q":"Which is correct: 'The yolk of the egg is white' or 'The yolk of the egg are white'?","o":["'Is white'","Neither: egg yolks are yellow","'Are white'","Both are fine"],"a":1,"e":"The grammar trap distracts you from the facts. 'Is' would be the right verb, but the yolk isn't white at all.","k":1},
  {"q":"Three boxes. One holds gold. Box A says 'The gold is here.' Box B says 'The gold is not here.' Box C says 'The gold is not in A.' Only one label is true. Where is the gold?","o":["Box B","Box A","Box C","It can't be worked out"],"a":0,"e":"Try each box. Gold in A makes the A and B labels true; gold in C makes the B and C labels true. Only gold in B leaves exactly one true label (C's)."},
  {"q":"Using only addition and exactly eight 8s, which of these makes 1,000?","o":["888 + 88 + 8 + 8 + 8 + 8","88 + 88 + 88 + 88 + 8 + 8","888 + 8 + 8 + 8 + 8 + 8","888 + 88 + 8 + 8 + 8"],"a":3,"e":"888 + 88 = 976, and three more 8s add 24 to reach exactly 1,000. Count the digits: 3 + 2 + 1 + 1 + 1 = 8."},
  {"q":"Divide 30 by a half, then add 10. What do you get?","o":["25","15","70","40"],"a":2,"e":"Dividing by a half means asking how many halves fit into 30, which is 60. Add 10 to get 70. Dividing by 2 would give 15 + 10 = 25."},
  {"q":"A leaky tap drips once every second. About 20 drops make 1 millilitre. Roughly how much water is wasted in a day?","o":["About 0.4 litres","About 4 litres","About 40 litres","About 400 litres"],"a":1,"e":"A day has 86,400 seconds, and 86,400 / 20 = 4,320 mL, or about 4.3 litres. Over a year that's more than 1,500 litres."},
  {"q":"You want to average 60 km/h over a 2 km lap. You drive the first kilometre at 30 km/h. How fast must you drive the second kilometre?","o":["It can't be done","90 km/h","120 km/h","180 km/h"],"a":0,"e":"Averaging 60 km/h over 2 km means finishing in 2 minutes. At 30 km/h the first kilometre alone takes 2 minutes, so there's no time left at all."},
  {"q":"A shirt costs $40 after a 20% discount. What was the original price?","o":["$48","$60","$45","$50"],"a":3,"e":"After 20% off you pay 80% of the original. If 80% is $40, then 10% is $5 and 100% is $50. Adding 20% to $40 gives the wrong answer of $48."},
  {"q":"A 100 kg watermelon is 99% water. After drying in the sun it is 98% water. What does it weigh now?","o":["98 kg","99 kg","50 kg","75 kg"],"a":2,"e":"The solid part is 1 kg and doesn't change. At first it's 1% of the weight; afterwards it's 2%, so the total must have halved to 50 kg."},
  {"q":"In a queue, you are 7th from the front and 7th from the back. How many people are in the queue?","o":["14","13","12","15"],"a":1,"e":"There are 6 people in front of you and 6 behind you, plus you: 13. Adding 7 + 7 counts you twice.","k":1},
  {"q":"Two volumes of an encyclopedia sit side by side on a shelf, in order, spines facing out. Each has 4 cm of pages and a 2 mm cover on each side. A bookworm chews from page 1 of Volume 1 to the last page of Volume 2. How far does it travel?","o":["4 mm","8 cm","8.4 cm","8.8 cm"],"a":0,"e":"With spines facing you, page 1 of Volume 1 sits on its right side, next to Volume 2, and the last page of Volume 2 sits on its left side. The worm only chews through two covers: 2 mm + 2 mm."},
  {"q":"On a standard die, opposite faces always add up to 7. If the top face shows 2, what's on the bottom?","o":["4","6","1","5"],"a":3,"e":"The pairs are 1 and 6, 2 and 5, and 3 and 4. So 2 on top means 5 underneath.","k":1},
  {"q":"A frog is 1 metre from a wall. Each jump carries it exactly half of the remaining distance. After how many jumps does it touch the wall?","o":["10","20","Never","100"],"a":2,"e":"After each jump some distance remains (a half, a quarter, an eighth ...), so it gets endlessly closer without arriving. After 10 jumps it's less than 1 mm away."},
  {"q":"Two people played 5 games of chess. Each won 3 games and there were no draws. How is that possible?","o":["One game was replayed","They didn't play each other","They counted a draw as a win","It isn't possible"],"a":1,"e":"Nothing says they were opponents. Each could have played other people, winning 3 and losing 2."},
  {"q":"You have 10 fingers. How many fingers are on 10 hands?","o":["50","100","10","20"],"a":0,"e":"Each hand has 5 fingers (counting the thumb), so 10 hands have 50. The mention of your 10 fingers is there to lure you into saying 100.","k":1},
  {"q":"Which is bigger: 2 multiplied by itself 10 times, or 10 multiplied by itself 3 times?","o":["10 multiplied by itself 3 times","They're exactly equal","It depends on the order","2 multiplied by itself 10 times"],"a":3,"e":"2 to the power of 10 is 1,024, and 10 x 10 x 10 is 1,000. That's why a 'kilobyte' was traditionally 1,024 bytes."},
  {"q":"Which fraction is larger: 3/7 or 4/9?","o":["3/7","They're equal","4/9","It can't be told without a calculator"],"a":2,"e":"Cross-multiply: 4 x 7 = 28 and 3 x 9 = 27, so 4/9 is a touch bigger. As decimals they're about 0.444 and 0.429."},
  {"q":"You draw one card from a shuffled 52-card deck. What is the chance it is a heart or a king (or both)?","o":["17 in 52","4 in 13","1 in 4","1 in 13"],"a":1,"e":"There are 13 hearts and 4 kings, but the king of hearts is in both groups. That's 16 different cards out of 52, which simplifies to 4 in 13."},
  {"q":"You roll two dice. What is the chance at least one of them shows a six?","o":["11 in 36","1 in 3","1 in 6","1 in 36"],"a":0,"e":"The chance neither shows a six is 5/6 x 5/6 = 25/36, so at least one six is 11/36. Adding 1/6 + 1/6 = 12/36 counts double sixes twice."},
  {"q":"On average, how many times do you need to roll a fair die to get your first six?","o":["3","3.5","36","6"],"a":3,"e":"When something has a 1 in 6 chance each try, it takes 6 tries on average. Some runs are much shorter and some much longer."},
  {"q":"A bag holds two coins: one normal and one with heads on both sides. You pick one at random, flip it, and it lands heads. What is the chance it's the double-headed coin?","o":["1 in 2","3 in 4","2 in 3","1 in 3"],"a":2,"e":"There are three equally likely heads you could be seeing: two from the double-headed coin and one from the normal coin. Two of those three belong to the double-headed coin."},
  {"q":"A knockout tennis tournament starts with 64 players. How many matches are played to find the champion?","o":["32","63","64","127"],"a":1,"e":"Every match knocks out exactly one player, and 63 players must be knocked out to leave one champion. The same trick works for any number of players, byes included."},
  {"q":"A chocolate bar is a 4 x 6 grid of squares. Each snap breaks one piece into two along a line. How many snaps are needed to get all 24 squares apart?","o":["23","10","24","20"],"a":0,"e":"Each snap adds exactly one piece. Going from 1 piece to 24 pieces therefore always takes 23 snaps, whatever order you break it in.","k":1},
  {"q":"You have 10 litres of juice that is 20% concentrate. How much water must you add to make it 10% concentrate?","o":["2 litres","5 litres","20 litres","10 litres"],"a":3,"e":"The mix contains 2 litres of concentrate. For that to be 10% of the drink, the total must be 20 litres, so add 10 litres of water."}
);
WB.SCENARIOS.push(
  {"topic":"Correlation vs causation","q":"Children from homes with lots of books tend to get better school results. A council plans to post free books to every family, expecting grades to jump. What's the flaw?","o":["Book-filled homes often differ in other ways, like parents' education and reading habits, so books alone may not cause the gap","None: more books will clearly raise grades","Books actually lower grades","Grades cause people to buy books, so it will backfire"],"a":0,"e":"A confounding variable can sit behind a correlation. A fair test would compare otherwise similar families, some given books and some not."},
  {"topic":"Correlation vs causation","q":"People who went to hospital this year were more likely to die this year than people who didn't. Should you avoid hospitals?","o":["Yes: hospitals are clearly dangerous","No: sicker people are the ones who go to hospital, so the illness explains the link","Yes, but only in winter","It proves hospitals cause illness"],"a":1,"e":"This is selection by illness, a form of confounding. Comparing patients with healthy people who stayed home is not a fair comparison."},
  {"topic":"Correlation vs causation","q":"The more firefighters sent to a fire, the more damage the fire tends to cause. What's the best explanation?","o":["Firefighters cause the damage","Fewer firefighters would reduce damage","Bigger fires cause both more damage and more firefighters being sent","It's pure coincidence"],"a":2,"e":"The size of the fire is a common cause of both. Mixing up which way cause runs, or missing a common cause, is a classic trap."},
  {"topic":"Correlation vs causation","q":"Suburbs with lots of trendy cafes have higher house prices. Will opening a new cafe on your street raise your home's value?","o":["Yes, cafes directly raise prices","Yes, by about 10%","No, cafes always lower prices","Unlikely on its own: wealthier areas attract both cafes and high prices"],"a":3,"e":"Local wealth is the likely common cause. Cafes tend to follow money into an area rather than create it."},
  {"topic":"Correlation vs causation","q":"Students who use a school's free tutoring get lower marks, on average, than students who don't. Does tutoring make students worse?","o":["Probably not: students who are already struggling are the ones who sign up","Yes, tutoring clearly harms learning","Yes, so the service should close","It proves the tutors are poorly trained"],"a":0,"e":"This is selection bias with possible reverse causation: low marks lead to tutoring, not the other way round. A fair test compares similar students."},
  {"topic":"Correlation vs causation","q":"People who carry a cigarette lighter are more likely to develop lung cancer. What explains this?","o":["Lighters give off cancer-causing fumes","Smoking: smokers are more likely to carry lighters and to develop lung cancer","Lung cancer makes people buy lighters","It shows lighters should be banned"],"a":1,"e":"Smoking is the confounding variable. The lighter is a marker of the real cause, not the cause itself."},
  {"topic":"Correlation vs causation","q":"Teams that got new standing desks report higher productivity, but those teams were also the newest and best-funded. What would best test whether the desks cause the boost?","o":["Ask the teams if they like the desks","Buy standing desks for everyone and see what happens","Randomly assign desks to some comparable teams and not others, then compare","Look only at the most productive team"],"a":2,"e":"Random assignment spreads other differences evenly between groups. It's the main way to turn a correlation into evidence of cause."},
  {"topic":"Correlation vs causation","q":"A study found countries that eat more chocolate per person have won more Nobel Prizes per person. What's the sensible takeaway?","o":["Chocolate makes people smarter","Winning Nobel Prizes makes people eat chocolate","Scientists should eat more chocolate","Wealthier countries tend to buy more chocolate and fund more research, so wealth may explain both"],"a":3,"e":"This real finding appeared in a medical journal in 2012 and is now a classic teaching example of spurious correlation. Comparing whole countries hides countless other differences."},
  {"topic":"Evidence quality","q":"A headline says a new compound 'cures cancer'. The study was done in mice. What's the best response?","o":["Be cautious: most treatments that work in mice don't work as well in people","Ask your doctor for it tomorrow","Accept it: mice and humans are very similar","Dismiss it as fake news"],"a":0,"e":"Animal studies are an early step, not proof. Only a small share of drugs that look promising in early research end up approved for people."},
  {"topic":"Evidence quality","q":"A gadget online has twelve 5-star reviews, all posted in the same week with very similar wording. What should you conclude?","o":["It must be excellent","The reviews may be fake or paid, so look for independent reviews elsewhere","Twelve reviews is plenty of evidence","Similar wording shows buyers agree"],"a":1,"e":"Clusters of glowing reviews with matching phrasing are a common sign of review manipulation. Independent sources carry more weight."},
  {"topic":"Evidence quality","q":"Which usually gives the most reliable overall picture of whether a treatment works?","o":["A single small trial with a striking result","A news story about a patient","A systematic review that pools many good-quality trials","An expert's personal opinion"],"a":2,"e":"Systematic reviews look at all the relevant studies, so one lucky or flawed result carries less weight."},
  {"topic":"Evidence quality","q":"An article claims 'a study shows' walking backwards boosts memory, but gives no link, author or journal. What's the smartest approach?","o":["Trust it: it mentions a study","Share it so others can decide","Assume the study is fake","Treat it as unverified until you can find and check the original study"],"a":3,"e":"A claim is only as good as its source. Many viral 'studies' turn out to be misreported, very small or nonexistent."},
  {"topic":"Evidence quality","q":"In a trial of a supplement for back pain, 70% of people felt better after six weeks. There was no comparison group. What's the problem?","o":["Back pain often improves on its own, so without a control group we can't tell if the supplement did anything","No problem: 70% is a strong result","Six weeks is too long","Supplements can't affect pain"],"a":0,"e":"A control group shows what would have happened anyway. Natural recovery and the placebo effect can make useless treatments look effective."},
  {"topic":"Evidence quality","q":"A study praising a breakfast cereal was funded by the company that makes it. What's the most sensible view?","o":["It must be false","It isn't automatically wrong, but look for independent studies that find the same thing","Funding never affects research","It's more trustworthy because the company knows the product best"],"a":1,"e":"Conflicts of interest raise the bar for evidence. Independent replication is the best check."},
  {"topic":"Probability","q":"A roulette wheel has landed on red eight times in a row. What are the chances of black on the next spin?","o":["Much higher, black is due","Lower, red is on a streak","The same as always: the wheel has no memory","Exactly 100%"],"a":2,"e":"Believing a result is 'due' is the gambler's fallacy. Each spin is independent, and the green zero means black is always slightly under 50%."},
  {"topic":"Probability","q":"A condition affects 1 in 1,000 people. A test catches every real case but also wrongly flags 5% of healthy people. You test positive. Roughly how likely is it you have the condition?","o":["About 95%","About 50%","Exactly 100%","About 2%"],"a":3,"e":"Out of 1,000 people, about 1 true case tests positive alongside about 50 false alarms, so only about 1 in 51. This is base rate neglect."},
  {"topic":"Probability","q":"The forecast said 30% chance of rain, and it stayed dry. Was the forecast wrong?","o":["No: it means on days like this, rain falls about 3 times in 10","Yes, completely wrong","Yes, it should have said 0%","It means 30% of the area gets rain for sure"],"a":0,"e":"A single outcome can't prove a probability wrong. Forecasts are judged by how well they match reality over many days."},
  {"topic":"Probability","q":"There are 23 people in a room. Roughly what's the chance at least two share a birthday?","o":["About 6%","About 50%","About 23%","Almost zero"],"a":1,"e":"This is the birthday paradox. With 23 people there are 253 possible pairs, which is why a match is far more likely than it feels."},
  {"topic":"Probability","q":"You roll a standard die four times. Roughly what's the chance of getting at least one six?","o":["About 67% (four times one-sixth)","About 17%","About 52%","100%"],"a":2,"e":"You can't simply add the chances. Work out the chance of no sixes, (5/6) to the power of 4, about 48%, and subtract from 100%."},
  {"topic":"Probability","q":"A raffle sells 1,000 tickets at $10 each for a single $5,000 prize. On average, what is each ticket worth?","o":["$10, you break even","$5,000","$50","$5, so you lose about $5 per ticket on average"],"a":3,"e":"Expected value is the prize times the chance of winning: $5,000 x 1/1,000 = $5. Fine for a good cause, but it's a donation, not an investment."},
  {"topic":"Probability","q":"A '1-in-100-year flood' hit a town last year. What's the chance of another one this year?","o":["Still about 1%: the label describes a yearly chance, not a schedule","Zero, it won't happen for 99 years","Higher, floods come in pairs","Exactly 100%"],"a":0,"e":"Over a 30-year home loan, a 1% yearly chance adds up to roughly a 26% chance of at least one such flood."},
  {"topic":"Probability","q":"You think of an old friend and, minutes later, they call you. What's the most likely explanation?","o":["You predicted it","Coincidence: with so many thoughts and calls over a lifetime, some will line up","Your friend sensed your thoughts","It proves the call was meant to be"],"a":1,"e":"The law of truly large numbers: with enough chances, rare coincidences become almost certain. We remember the hits and forget the misses."},
  {"topic":"Logical fallacies","q":"Sam: \"We should cut back on sugary drinks in school canteens.\" Alex: \"So you want kids never to enjoy a treat again?\" What has Alex done?","o":["A fair summary","An ad hominem","A straw man: misrepresenting Sam's point to make it easier to attack","An appeal to popularity"],"a":2,"e":"A straw man swaps the real argument for a weaker, exaggerated one. Restating someone's view fairly is the first step to disagreeing well."},
  {"topic":"Logical fallacies","q":"\"Nobody has proven this fuel-saving gadget doesn't work, so it must work.\" What's wrong with this?","o":["Nothing, it's sound reasoning","It's a slippery slope","It's a false dilemma","It's an appeal to ignorance: a lack of disproof isn't proof"],"a":3,"e":"The burden of proof sits with whoever makes the claim. Independent tests of many fuel-saving gadgets have found little or no benefit."},
  {"topic":"Logical fallacies","q":"\"I started taking vitamin C, and a week later my cold was gone. So it cured my cold.\" What's the flaw?","o":["Post hoc reasoning: most colds clear up in about a week anyway","No flaw: the timing proves it","It's a straw man","It's circular reasoning"],"a":0,"e":"Post hoc ergo propter hoc means 'after this, therefore because of this'. Something happening first doesn't mean it caused what followed."},
  {"topic":"Bias","q":"After several news stories about home break-ins, you guess burglary is far more common than official figures show. What's likely going on?","o":["Survivorship bias","The availability heuristic: vivid, recent examples feel more common than they are","The figures must be wrong","Anchoring"],"a":1,"e":"We judge how likely something is by how easily examples come to mind. News coverage makes rare events very easy to recall."},
  {"topic":"Bias","q":"A surgeon says an operation has a '90% survival rate'. Another says it has a '10% death rate'. Many patients prefer the first. Why?","o":["The first operation is safer","The second surgeon is less skilled","The framing effect: the same fact feels different depending on how it's worded","Confirmation bias"],"a":2,"e":"Both statements are identical. Try restating a figure the other way round before deciding."},
  {"topic":"Bias","q":"Most people stay in whatever super investment option they were put in when they started a job, without ever checking it. What explains this?","o":["Defaults are always the best choice","The halo effect","Hindsight bias","Status quo bias: we tend to stick with the default"],"a":3,"e":"Defaults are powerful because changing takes effort. Checking fees and options occasionally can make a real difference over decades."},
  {"topic":"Bias","q":"After the share market drops, a friend says, \"I knew that was going to happen.\" They didn't sell anything beforehand. What's this?","o":["Hindsight bias: past events seem more predictable than they really were","Good forecasting","Loss aversion","The framing effect"],"a":0,"e":"Once we know the outcome, we rewrite our memory of how certain we felt. A useful check: did they act on it at the time?"},
  {"topic":"Bias","q":"An interviewer is impressed by a candidate's confident, polished manner and rates their spreadsheet skills highly too, without testing them. What's the bias?","o":["Anchoring","The halo effect: one good trait colours judgements of unrelated ones","Survivorship bias","No bias: confident people are skilled"],"a":1,"e":"The halo effect is why structured interviews and practical tests are used. They judge each skill on its own evidence."},
  {"topic":"Bias","q":"Surveys often find most drivers rate themselves as above average. What does this show?","o":["Most drivers really are above average","The surveys are rigged","Overconfidence: most people can't all be above average","The availability heuristic"],"a":2,"e":"This is sometimes called illusory superiority. We know our own good intentions but judge others by their mistakes."},
  {"topic":"Statistics","q":"An ad says a medicine 'halves your risk of a heart attack'. The risk falls from 2 in 1,000 to 1 in 1,000. What's the fuller picture?","o":["Half of all heart attacks are prevented","Your risk falls by 50 percentage points","It prevents 500 heart attacks per 1,000 people","It's a 50% relative drop, but only 1 fewer case per 1,000 people"],"a":3,"e":"Relative risk sounds dramatic; absolute risk shows the real size of the change. Always ask 'out of how many?'"},
  {"topic":"Statistics","q":"A company's bar chart shows its profits towering over a rival's. The vertical axis starts at 95 instead of 0. What should you notice?","o":["The cut-off axis exaggerates what may be a small difference","The company is far more profitable","Charts can't be misleading","Axes always start at 95"],"a":0,"e":"A truncated axis magnifies small gaps. Check the numbers on the axis, not just the height of the bars."},
  {"topic":"Statistics","q":"A poll shows 51% support for a plan and 49% against, with a margin of error of plus or minus 3 points. What's the best conclusion?","o":["The plan clearly has majority support","It's too close to call: the real split could easily go either way","The poll is useless","Exactly 51% of people support it"],"a":1,"e":"The margin of error means support could plausibly be anywhere from about 48% to 54%. Small leads inside it are not meaningful."},
  {"topic":"Statistics","q":"A loan's interest rate rises from 4% to 5%. Which description is accurate?","o":["It's up 1%","It's up 5%","It's up 1 percentage point, which is a 25% increase in the rate","It's up 25 percentage points"],"a":2,"e":"Percentage points measure the gap between two percentages; per cent measures relative change. Headlines often mix them up."},
  {"topic":"Statistics","q":"A stat says most car crashes happen within 5 km of home. Does that mean driving near home is especially dangerous?","o":["Yes, local roads are the most dangerous","Yes, people drive worse near home","It means long trips have no crashes","Not necessarily: most driving happens near home, so most crashes will too"],"a":3,"e":"This is a denominator problem. You'd need crashes per kilometre driven to compare how risky each kind of trip is."},
  {"topic":"Decision making","q":"You could drive 40 minutes each way across town to save $20 on a toaster. What's the smartest way to think about it?","o":["Count the fuel and your time too: the real saving may be small or nothing","Always go: $20 is $20","Never go: saving money is pointless","Buy two toasters"],"a":0,"e":"This is opportunity cost: every choice uses time and money that could go elsewhere. Price alone rarely tells the whole story."},
  {"topic":"Decision making","q":"You're choosing a paint colour for a room, and separately deciding whether to sell your house. How should your effort differ?","o":["Treat both exactly the same","Spend far more care on the decision that's hard to undo","Spend most time on the paint","Decide both on gut feel in seconds"],"a":1,"e":"Reversible decisions can be made quickly and fixed later. Irreversible ones deserve slow, careful thought."},
  {"topic":"Decision making","q":"Your team is about to launch a project and everyone is upbeat. What's a good technique to uncover hidden problems?","o":["Ask only the most positive person","Skip planning to save time","A pre-mortem: imagine the project has failed, then list the reasons why","Launch first and check later"],"a":2,"e":"Imagining failure makes it easier to voice doubts that optimism hides. Psychologist Gary Klein popularised the method."},
  {"topic":"Decision making","q":"You've spent three weeks comparing 40 kettles and still can't choose. What's the most sensible approach?","o":["Keep researching until you find the perfect one","Buy all 40","Give up on kettles","Set a few must-have criteria and buy the first one that meets them"],"a":3,"e":"This is satisficing: choosing 'good enough' instead of the best possible. Research suggests satisficers are often happier with their choices."},
  {"topic":"Decision making","q":"At the cinema: small popcorn $5, medium $8.50, large $9. What's the medium most likely there to do?","o":["Make the large look like a bargain so more people buy it","Give the best value","Suit most people's appetite","Nothing, it's just a size"],"a":0,"e":"This is the decoy effect. Decide how much you actually want before comparing prices."},
  {"topic":"Decision making","q":"After a heated argument with your manager, you want to email your resignation right now. What's the smartest move?","o":["Send it immediately while you feel strongly","Wait until you're calm, then decide","Post about it online first","Resign by text instead"],"a":1,"e":"Strong emotion narrows thinking and makes the present feel permanent. Big, hard-to-reverse choices are best made with a cool head."},
  {"topic":"Decision making","q":"In a meeting, the boss states their view first and everyone quickly agrees. How could the group make a better decision?","o":["Let the boss decide alone","Take a vote after the boss speaks","Have people write down their views independently before anyone discusses","Only invite people who agree"],"a":2,"e":"This helps avoid groupthink, where the wish for harmony overrides honest assessment. Early, independent views bring more information to the table."},
  {"topic":"Decision making","q":"You want to save more but always seem to spend what's in your account. What's a proven strategy?","o":["Rely on willpower each week","Save whatever is left at the end of the month","Check your balance less often","Set up an automatic transfer to savings on payday"],"a":3,"e":"This is a commitment device: deciding once, in advance, removes the daily temptation. 'Pay yourself first' is the classic version."},
  {"topic":"Decision making","q":"You're offered either a guaranteed $500 or a coin flip for $1,200 or nothing. Which has the higher expected value?","o":["The coin flip: an average of $600 per go","The guaranteed $500","They're exactly equal","Neither has an expected value"],"a":0,"e":"Expected value is each outcome times its chance: 50% of $1,200 is $600. Many people still take the sure thing, which is reasonable if you can't afford to get nothing."},
  {"topic":"Scams","q":"A caller says they're from the ATO, that you owe tax, and that police will arrest you today unless you pay in gift cards. What should you do?","o":["Buy the gift cards to be safe","Hang up and contact the ATO using the number on its official website","Give your tax file number to confirm your identity","Ask them to call back tomorrow"],"a":1,"e":"The ATO doesn't threaten immediate arrest or ask for payment in gift cards or cryptocurrency. Pressure and unusual payment methods are hallmarks of a scam."},
  {"topic":"Scams","q":"You get a text saying your myGov account is suspended, with a link to 'verify your details'. What's the smartest response?","o":["Tap it quickly before the account is deleted","Reply STOP with your details","Don't tap it: myGov says it won't send links to sign in, so open the app or type the address yourself","Forward it to family to check"],"a":2,"e":"Government impersonation texts rely on fear and urgency. Going to the official site yourself sidesteps the fake link entirely."},
  {"topic":"Scams","q":"A text says Australia Post couldn't deliver your parcel and asks you to pay a $2.99 redelivery fee through a link. What's really going on?","o":["A normal delivery fee","A way to track your parcel","Harmless, since it's only $2.99","It's likely a scam aimed at stealing your card details"],"a":3,"e":"The small fee lowers your guard. The real goal is your card number, which can then be used for much bigger purchases."},
  {"topic":"Scams","q":"A message from an unknown number says, \"Hi Mum, I dropped my phone, this is my new number. Can you pay a bill for me urgently?\" What's the safest move?","o":["Call your child on their old number or check in person before doing anything","Pay it: they sound stressed","Reply asking which bill","Transfer a smaller amount first"],"a":0,"e":"This 'Hi Mum' scam relies on love and urgency. Verifying through a channel you already trust stops it cold."},
  {"topic":"Scams","q":"An online ad shows a famous Australian endorsing a crypto platform that 'guarantees' 20% returns a month. What's the smartest conclusion?","o":["A rare opportunity worth trying","Treat it as a scam: guaranteed high returns are a major red flag, and celebrity videos can be faked","Safe, because a celebrity backs it","Fine if you only invest a little"],"a":1,"e":"Scammers use fake news pages and deepfake videos of well-known people. ASIC's Moneysmart website lists common warning signs and companies to avoid."},
  {"topic":"Scams","q":"A pop-up on your computer says it's infected and tells you to call a 'Microsoft support' number. What should you do?","o":["Call and let them fix it remotely","Pay for the virus removal they offer","Close the browser and never call the number or allow remote access","Enter your password to unlock it"],"a":2,"e":"Real tech companies don't use pop-ups asking you to call them. Remote access lets scammers into your bank accounts and files."},
  {"topic":"Scams","q":"At work, a supplier emails saying their bank details have changed and asks you to pay the next invoice to the new account. What's the safest step?","o":["Reply to the email to confirm","Pay it, since the email looks genuine","Pay half to each account","Phone the supplier on a number you already have to confirm before paying"],"a":3,"e":"This is business email compromise. Scammers hack or copy real email accounts, so the check must use a separate, trusted channel."},
  {"topic":"Scams","q":"A cold caller offers to help you access your super early to pay off debts, for a fee. What's the smartest response?","o":["Decline: super can only be released early in limited legal circumstances, and these schemes can cost you your savings","Accept, since it's your money","Give them your fund details to check eligibility","Ask for a discount on the fee"],"a":0,"e":"Illegal early release schemes can leave you with drained super and tax penalties. Your fund or the ATO can explain the genuine rules."},
  {"topic":"Scams","q":"You're selling a couch online. A buyer says they've paid by PayID, then sends an email saying you must pay a fee to 'upgrade to a business account' before you receive it. What's happening?","o":["A normal bank process","It's a scam: PayID doesn't require sellers to pay anything to receive money","The buyer is being generous","You should pay, then ask for a refund"],"a":1,"e":"Fake PayID emails are a common marketplace scam. Check your actual bank account for the money before handing anything over."},
  {"topic":"Money sense","q":"A credit card has a $3,000 balance at 20% interest a year. If the balance stayed the same all year, roughly how much interest would you pay?","o":["About $60","About $200","About $600","About $3,000"],"a":2,"e":"20% of $3,000 is $600. Paying only the minimum each month means much of each payment goes to interest, so debts can take years to clear."},
  {"topic":"Money sense","q":"Someone has $5,000 in savings earning 4.5% and a $5,000 credit card debt at 20%. In general terms, what does paying off the card do?","o":["Costs more than it saves","Makes no difference","Saves about $225 a year","Saves about $1,000 a year in interest while giving up about $225 in savings interest"],"a":3,"e":"Paying off high-interest debt is like earning a guaranteed return equal to its rate. Many people keep a small emergency buffer as well."},
  {"topic":"Money sense","q":"Your savings earn 2% interest a year while prices rise 4% a year. What's happening to what your money can buy?","o":["It's shrinking by roughly 2% a year","It's growing by 2% a year","It's growing by 6% a year","It stays exactly the same"],"a":0,"e":"The real return is roughly interest minus inflation. A bigger number in your account doesn't always mean more buying power."},
  {"topic":"Money sense","q":"Two home loans advertise 5.8% and 5.9% interest. The 5.8% loan has much higher fees. What helps compare them fairly?","o":["The advertised rate alone","The comparison rate, which combines interest and most fees into one figure","The lender's logo","The length of the ad"],"a":1,"e":"Australian lenders must show a comparison rate next to advertised rates. It's based on a standard loan amount, so your own costs may differ a little."},
  {"topic":"Money sense","q":"Super fund A charges 1% of your balance a year in fees; fund B charges 0.5%. On a $100,000 balance, what's the yearly difference?","o":["$50","$5","$500","$5,000"],"a":2,"e":"1% is $1,000 and 0.5% is $500. Over decades, that gap also misses out on compounding, so small fee differences can grow large."},
  {"topic":"Money sense","q":"Rice costs $4.00 for a 500 g bag or $5.40 for a 750 g bag. Which is cheaper per kilogram?","o":["The 500 g bag","They cost the same per kg","You can't compare them","The 750 g bag, at $7.20 per kg versus $8.00"],"a":3,"e":"Unit pricing shows cost per 100 g or per kg, and big Australian supermarkets must display it. Bigger packs are often, but not always, cheaper per unit."},
  {"topic":"Money sense","q":"An investment of $100 falls 10%, then rises 10%. What is it worth now?","o":["$99","$100","$101","$110"],"a":0,"e":"$100 falls to $90, and 10% of $90 is only $9. A percentage fall needs a bigger percentage rise to recover."},
  {"topic":"Money sense","q":"Instead of paying a loan monthly, someone pays half the monthly amount every fortnight. Why does this usually pay the loan off faster?","o":["Fortnightly payments have no interest","There are 26 fortnights a year, so they make the equivalent of 13 monthly payments instead of 12","Banks give a fortnightly discount","It doesn't make any difference"],"a":1,"e":"The extra payment each year goes straight to reducing the balance, which also cuts future interest. It's a common general strategy, not a lender trick."},
  {"topic":"Risk","q":"Choosing car insurance, you can lower your premium by picking a higher excess. What's the key question to ask?","o":["Which option has the lowest premium, full stop?","Which insurer has the best ads?","Could I comfortably pay that excess if I needed to claim?","Can I avoid ever claiming?"],"a":2,"e":"The excess is what you pay towards each claim. A higher excess trades a lower regular cost for a bigger bill when something goes wrong."},
  {"topic":"Risk","q":"After news of a plane crash, a friend decides to drive interstate instead of flying 'to be safe'. What's the reality?","o":["Driving is much safer","They are about equally safe","Flying risk doubles after a crash","Per kilometre, driving is far riskier than flying on a commercial airline"],"a":3,"e":"Rare, dramatic events feel more dangerous than everyday ones. Road crashes kill over 1,000 Australians a year, far more than air travel."},
  {"topic":"Risk","q":"Someone keeps all their savings in shares of one company they love. What's the main risk?","o":["If that single company struggles, they could lose a large part of their savings","No risk if the company is well known","Shares can't lose value","Too much diversification"],"a":0,"e":"Diversification, spreading money across many investments, reduces the damage any single failure can do. It lowers risk without needing to pick winners."},
  {"topic":"Risk","q":"When insuring your home building, which figure should you base the sum insured on?","o":["What the house would sell for","The full cost to rebuild, including demolition and professional fees","What you paid for it years ago","The land value"],"a":1,"e":"Sale prices include land, which doesn't burn down, and rebuilding costs change over time. Underinsurance often comes to light only after a disaster."},
  {"topic":"Risk","q":"At a patrolled Australian beach, where is the safest place to swim?","o":["Wherever the water looks calmest","Just outside the flags where it's quieter","Between the red and yellow flags","Near the rocks for shelter"],"a":2,"e":"Calm-looking patches can be rip currents, one of the biggest coastal hazards. Lifesavers set the flags where they can watch closely."},
  {"topic":"Risk","q":"Why is an emergency fund, such as a few months of expenses in an accessible account, often recommended?","o":["It earns the highest possible returns","Banks require it","It replaces the need for any insurance","It covers surprises like job loss or repairs without needing high-interest debt"],"a":3,"e":"An emergency fund is a buffer against uncertainty. Its job is to be available when needed, not to grow fast."},
  {"topic":"Risk","q":"A friend skips travel insurance for an overseas trip because 'nothing went wrong last time'. What's the flaw?","o":["Past luck doesn't change the risk, and one medical emergency overseas can cost a fortune","No flaw: past trips predict future ones","Insurance only covers lost luggage","Nothing ever goes wrong overseas"],"a":0,"e":"Insurance is for low-chance, high-cost events. Hospital bills and medical evacuations abroad can run into tens of thousands of dollars or more."},
  {"topic":"Risk","q":"On a day of catastrophic bushfire danger, what do Australian fire authorities advise people in high-risk areas to do?","o":["Wait to see smoke before deciding","Leave early, before a fire starts","Stay and defend any home","Drive towards the fire to check it"],"a":1,"e":"Many deaths happen during late evacuations. A written bushfire plan made in advance means you act before panic sets in."},
  {"topic":"Risk","q":"The UV index is 3 on a cool, cloudy day. Is sun protection needed?","o":["No, clouds block all UV","No, only hot days matter","Yes: sun protection is recommended when the UV index is 3 or above, whatever the temperature","Only at the beach"],"a":2,"e":"UV isn't heat, so cool days can still burn. Checking the daily UV index is more reliable than going by how warm it feels."}
);
